00:09:59,267 --> 00:10:00,967
- Big, bold, and brassy.

246
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,033
- [Narrator] And
there goes Mike.

247
00:10:03,067 --> 00:10:05,033
- [Joel] Now, is that
really an efficient way

248
00:10:05,067 --> 00:10:06,567
to bring food to market?

249
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:08,067
- [Tom Servo] I don't think so.

250
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:09,000
- [Narrator] Too bad.

251
00:10:09,033 --> 00:10:09,800
Better luck next time, kid.

252
00:10:09,833 --> 00:10:11,300
- Loser.

253
00:10:11,333 --> 00:10:13,967
- [Narrator] Sam Wood, champion
calf roper of junior high.

254
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,800
- You know, the ironic
thing is Sam's a vegetarian,

255
00:10:16,833 --> 00:10:21,033
but there's nothing exciting
about roping okra patty.

256
00:10:22,067 --> 00:10:23,067
- [Narrator] Put
him down, fella.

257
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:24,867
- You were adopted.

258
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:30,167
- Now it's the calf
truss up competition.

259
00:10:31,533 --> 00:10:33,133
- [Narrator] 26 seconds.

260
00:10:33,167 --> 00:10:36,700
- [Tom Servo] Yeah, I'm
hot, that's me pretty much.

261
00:10:36,733 --> 00:10:39,000
- [Joel] We'll never
escape this town.

262
00:10:39,033 --> 00:10:41,967
- [Narrator] Jim Rand, champion
calf roper of senior high.

263
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,667
- And next year's janitor.

264
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:45,200
- [Narrator] Off his
horse in a flash,

265
00:10:45,233 --> 00:10:48,067
just like his dad
in a big-time rodeo.

266
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:50,467
- [Tom Servo] Holding up the
legacy of secrets and shame.

267
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,700
- You know, some
scars don't heal.

268
00:10:52,733 --> 00:10:54,633
Up next is another young feller.

269
00:10:54,667 --> 00:10:56,333
- [Narrator] 16 seconds.

270
00:10:56,367 --> 00:10:57,967
- [Joel] And the
crowd goes wild.

271
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,067
- [All] Yay.

272
00:11:00,067 --> 00:11:01,533
- [Narrator] Calf
riders, stand by.

273
00:11:01,567 --> 00:11:03,967
- [Joel] I don't really
know what I'm doing!

274
00:11:04,067 --> 00:11:05,167
Oh!

275
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:07,200
- Why can't Johnny ride?

276
00:11:13,433 --> 00:11:15,500
- [Narrator] Here comes Bobby,

277
00:11:15,533 --> 00:11:17,067
and there he goes.

278
00:11:17,067 --> 00:11:20,533
- [Joel] Fortunately, Bobby
didn't need his spine.

279
00:11:20,567 --> 00:11:22,500
- [Narrator] The girls
aren't bashful, either.

280
00:11:22,533 --> 00:11:25,067
- Like my friend Geeky,
that which does not kill you

281
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:26,833
makes you stronger.

282
00:11:28,767 --> 00:11:30,067
- [Tom Servo] Oh,
here comes this guy.

283
00:11:30,067 --> 00:11:30,800
- [Narrator] And there
goes the youngest

284
00:11:30,833 --> 00:11:32,167
bull rider of them all.

285
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:35,300
- [Tom Servo] Ow, dat der
drat it, aw gee criminy.

286
00:11:35,333 --> 00:11:38,667
- [Joel] Go ahead, strip me
of my dignity at age four.

287
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:40,700
What are you looking at!

288
00:11:42,267 --> 00:11:44,667
- [Narrator] Kind of
tall for a calf rider.

289
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:46,367
- Not any more.

290
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:47,567
- [Narrator] Bettie's
in high school.

291
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:48,867
- [Joel] Used to be.

292
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:49,867
- [Narrator] You
should see the boys

293
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:51,833
when she goes square dancing.

294
00:11:51,867 --> 00:11:55,333
- [Tom Servo] Yeah, they
buck her off right away.

295
00:12:03,567 --> 00:12:06,067
- Oh, she can't take a dare.

296
00:12:08,667 --> 00:12:11,867
- [Joel] Anybody get
a license number?

297
00:12:15,167 --> 00:12:17,067
- [Narrator] It's all
the same for Mary Lou,

298
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:19,500
beauty contest or calf riding.

299
00:12:19,533 --> 00:12:20,800
- [Tom Servo] Uh,
oatmeal's done.

300
00:12:20,833 --> 00:12:22,667
- [Narrator] She
can win 'em both.

301
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:23,800
Yes sir.

302
00:12:23,833 --> 00:12:24,700
- Yes, yes.

303
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:30,067
- [Joel] Hey honey, want to
come to my pen for drinks later?

304
00:12:30,067 --> 00:12:31,167
And the crowd goes wild.

305
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:32,200
- [All] Yay.

306
00:12:35,233 --> 00:12:37,167
- [Narrator] Senior
high bull riding.

307
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:38,233
- [Tom Servo] Fresh
off the grill.

308
00:12:38,267 --> 00:12:39,967
- [Narrator] This Angus
bull is no hobby horse.

309
00:12:40,067 --> 00:12:41,733
- No, it's garbage.

310
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:45,567
- [Tom Servo] He's
a moveable feast.

311
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:51,300
- [Narrator] Will you look
at that bull, he's really--

312
00:12:51,333 --> 00:12:54,667
- [Tom Servo] Oh, take
the rope off my groin.

313
00:13:01,067 --> 00:13:03,367
- [Joel] Here comes the
last surviving castrati.

314
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:06,200
- [Narrator] Only two years
ago he was still riding calves.

315
00:13:06,233 --> 00:13:08,867
- Now he sells pencil and
string on street corners.

316
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:11,333
- [Narrator] Dad's going to
be mighty proud of him today.

317
00:13:11,367 --> 00:13:13,067
- If he can find him.

318
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:21,500
Oh no, oh no, this is wrong.

319
00:13:21,533 --> 00:13:24,067
I signed up for debate!

320
00:13:24,067 --> 00:13:25,067
- [Narrator] Just six seconds.

321
00:13:25,067 --> 00:13:26,533
- [Tom Servo] Over Tokyo.

322
00:13:26,567 --> 00:13:28,300
- [Narrator] And here's
Slash Barcay's meanest bronc.

323
00:13:28,333 --> 00:13:30,733
- That horse has
got Air Jordan's on.

324
00:13:30,767 --> 00:13:32,900
- [Narrator] Sits him like
he was glued to the saddle.

325
00:13:32,933 --> 00:13:33,733
- I am!

326
00:13:36,833 --> 00:13:40,833
- [Tom Servo] Hey, hey,
you're crowding my lane.

327
00:13:40,867 --> 00:13:42,167
- [Joel] And the
crowd goes wild.

328
00:13:42,200 --> 00:13:43,733
- [All] Yay.

329
00:13:43,767 --> 00:13:45,067
- [Narrator] Okay
Glen, get out there

330
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:46,167
and show them how to do it.

331
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:49,067
- [Joel] Or don't
come home tonight.

332
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:53,333
- I am not an animal.

333
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:57,533
- [Narrator] Watch out,
that ground's awful hard.

334
00:13:57,567 --> 00:13:59,333
- [Joel] Life is awful hard.

335
00:13:59,367 --> 00:14:01,800
- [Narrator] We sure
had us a junior rodeo.

336
00:14:01,833 --> 00:14:03,667
- Next week, geriatric rodeo.

337
00:14:03,700 --> 00:14:05,867
- [Narrator] And then it
came prize giving time.

338
00:14:05,900 --> 00:14:09,100
- [Tom Servo] First
price, bronze cow chips.

339
00:14:10,267 --> 00:14:11,967
- [Narrator] Prizes for winning.

340
00:14:12,067 --> 00:14:13,167
- Even George F.
Kaufman won a prize.

341
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:15,067
- [Narrator] And
prizes for trying.

342
00:14:15,067 --> 00:14:18,967
- [Joel] In other
words, bitter failure.

343
00:14:19,067 --> 00:14:20,367
- It's Hayley Mills.

344
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:22,067
- Sure loved you
in The Parent Trap.

345
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:25,800
- [Narrator] Yes sirree, the
junior rodeo is here to stay,

346
00:14:25,833 --> 00:14:30,067
and nobody's happier about it
than old-timer Billy Slater.

347
00:14:31,267 --> 00:14:32,800
- [Tom Servo] It's sad, really.

348
00:14:32,833 --> 00:14:37,067
* And the guys are
not clowning all day

349
00:14:40,067 --> 00:14:42,733
(upbeat music)

350
00:14:47,433 --> 00:14:49,900
- [Narrator] Those
who hunt by night

351
00:14:49,933 --> 00:14:52,833
will tell you that the
wildest and most vicious

352
00:14:52,867 --> 00:14:54,200
of all animals--

353
00:14:54,233 --> 00:14:55,133
- [Tom Servo] Are in Vegas?

354
00:14:55,167 --> 00:14:56,467
- [Narrator] Is the tiny shrew.

355
00:14:56,500 --> 00:14:57,800
- [All] A shrew?

356
00:14:57,833 --> 00:15:01,167
- [Narrator] Feeds only
by the dark of the moon.

357
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:02,733
- Yeah, just go with
us on this, okay?

358
00:15:02,767 --> 00:15:04,467
- [Narrator] He must
eat his own body weight

359
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:06,167
every few hours.

360
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:07,467
- [Tom Servo] Plus
a delicious shake.

361
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:11,333
- [Narrator] And the
shrew devours everything,

362
00:15:11,367 --> 00:15:14,067
bones, flesh,
marrow, everything.

363
00:15:15,700 --> 00:15:17,067
- He can really
tuck away a moth.

364
00:15:17,067 --> 00:15:21,067
- [Narrator] First in
Alaska, and then invading

365
00:15:21,067 --> 00:15:25,067
steadily southward, there
were reports of a new species.

366
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:27,400
- [Joel] A fun species.

367
00:15:27,433 --> 00:15:29,867
- [Narrator] The
giant killer shrew.

368
00:15:29,900 --> 00:15:30,833
- So there!

369
00:15:32,067 --> 00:15:33,567
- The.
- The.

370
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:34,967
- Killer.
- Killer.

371
00:15:35,067 --> 00:15:35,967
- Shrews.
- Shrews.

372
00:15:36,067 --> 00:15:39,900
- Starring Joan Collins
and Jackie Collins.

373
00:15:39,933 --> 00:15:42,833
- [Joel] Oh, Ingrid is
good, but James is best.

374
00:15:42,867 --> 00:15:44,333
- [Tom Servo] I think so.

375
00:15:50,833 --> 00:15:53,167
- [Joel] Based on a
novel by William Shatner.

376
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:54,467
Spock.

377
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:56,733
- Oh, the great
William M. Cline.

378
00:15:56,767 --> 00:15:58,200
Wilfred (chuckles).

379
00:15:58,233 --> 00:16:00,233
- [Tom Servo] Oh, I passed
a blue stone last night,

380
00:16:00,267 --> 00:16:02,067
let me tell you.

381
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:03,367
- [Joel] No shrews were hurt

382
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:05,067
during the making
of this picture.

383
00:16:05,067 --> 00:16:07,167
- [Tom Servo] Well, a few, but.

384
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:10,833
Big Ben Chapman.

385
00:16:13,067 --> 00:16:15,733
- Matthew, if you ain't
the orneriest sprout

386
00:16:15,767 --> 00:16:17,667
that ever jumped on my lawn.

387
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:19,067
- [Joel] Hey, Ray Kellogg,

388
00:16:19,067 --> 00:16:21,533
he directed The
Giant Gila Monster.

389
00:16:21,567 --> 00:16:22,867
- [Tom Servo] No, no!

390
00:16:26,067 --> 00:16:26,967
Oh, it's Robert Maxwell's boat.

391
00:16:27,067 --> 00:16:29,067
Mr. Maxwell, Mr. Maxwell.

392
00:16:30,833 --> 00:16:33,700
- [Joel] Hey, it's PT
90210, check it out.

393
00:16:33,733 --> 00:16:36,067
- Sure is a lot of
quiet out there.

394
00:16:36,067 --> 00:16:37,167
- Too lot of quiet.

395
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:39,067
- Almost smell it, can't you?

396
00:16:39,100 --> 00:16:39,867
- [Joel] No, that's me.

397
00:16:39,900 --> 00:16:41,700
- No, but I can feel it.

398
00:16:41,733 --> 00:16:42,700
The pressure's dropped so fast,

399
00:16:42,733 --> 00:16:45,067
it's almost made my ears pop.

400
00:16:45,067 --> 00:16:46,533
- [Joel] Chew gum.

401
00:16:46,567 --> 00:16:50,133
- Hurricane's got definite
ways of telling you it's there.

402
00:16:50,167 --> 00:16:51,500
Where is it?

403
00:16:51,533 --> 00:16:52,667
- I thought you knew.

404
00:16:52,700 --> 00:16:53,900
- Dead ahead.

405
00:16:53,933 --> 00:16:55,133
- [Joel] Do you think I'm heavy?

406
00:16:55,167 --> 00:16:56,967
- What's that mean to us.

407
00:16:57,067 --> 00:16:58,700
- Now boy, that puts our port
right in the middle of it.

408
00:16:58,733 --> 00:17:00,100
- Oh God help me.

409
00:17:04,067 --> 00:17:05,667
- There's a cove on
the lee side, though.

410
00:17:05,700 --> 00:17:07,500
We'll snuggle up close to
the bluff and ride it out.

411
00:17:07,533 --> 00:17:09,733
- We most certainly will not.

412
00:17:09,767 --> 00:17:14,067
- Well, the chart shows it's
good holding ground, it's clay.

413
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:16,067
- [Joel] Yep.

414
00:17:16,100 --> 00:17:17,067
- You know something?

415
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:18,167
- [Joel] What?

416
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:19,467
- If this thing had
an automatic pilot,

417
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:20,700
I wouldn't have to
put up with you.

418
00:17:20,733 --> 00:17:22,533
- Then you wouldn't
have nobody to chew out.

419
00:17:22,567 --> 00:17:25,067
- [Tom Servo] Yeah, wait til
the whip comes down, white boy.

420
00:17:25,067 --> 00:17:26,667
- Be there in a couple of hours,

421
00:17:26,700 --> 00:17:28,833
these mills of
yours keep running.

422
00:17:28,867 --> 00:17:32,067
- Look, automatic pilots
can't play dixieland jazz

423
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:34,167
on their banjos like I can.

424
00:17:34,200 --> 00:17:35,200
- (laughs) Good point.

425
00:17:35,233 --> 00:17:38,067
- What the heck did that mean?

426
00:17:38,067 --> 00:17:39,733
- [Joel] They're trolling
kind of fast, aren't they?

427
00:17:39,767 --> 00:17:42,367
- [Tom Servo] They're
gonna lose bait.

428
00:17:44,067 --> 00:17:45,067
- Hey, Rook.

429
00:17:45,067 --> 00:17:46,233
Rook, come here.

430
00:17:46,267 --> 00:17:48,900
- These things make
everything look bigger!

431
00:17:48,933 --> 00:17:50,167
- Take a look.

432
00:17:51,067 --> 00:17:53,067
There she is, dead ahead.

433
00:17:53,067 --> 00:17:57,067
- Oh yeah, it's Gilligan's
Island, the headhunter episode.

434
00:17:57,100 --> 00:18:00,133
- Man, that sure do look good.

435
00:18:00,167 --> 00:18:01,700
- [Joel] Mrs. Howell?

436
00:18:01,733 --> 00:18:03,800
- Man named Craigis owns
the island, does he?

437
00:18:03,833 --> 00:18:05,467
- That's the name on the
crate, Dr. Milo Craigis.

438
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:07,800
- We'll unload tomorrow.

439
00:18:07,833 --> 00:18:09,400
I don't want this craft
sitting high in the water

440
00:18:09,433 --> 00:18:10,733
when that blow hits.

441
00:18:10,767 --> 00:18:11,800
- [Tom Servo] Yes, yes.

442
00:18:11,833 --> 00:18:12,967
- With a lot of water under?

443
00:18:13,067 --> 00:18:14,067
- That's right.

444
00:18:14,067 --> 00:18:15,800
That sea won't pick
up in that cove,

445
00:18:15,833 --> 00:18:19,167
that wind'll have it
whipping around like a kite.

446
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:21,400
Step forward and break out
the heavy weather hook.

447
00:18:21,433 --> 00:18:23,067
Check and make sure
it's free to run.

448
00:18:23,067 --> 00:18:25,733
- Yeah, but a heavy weather
hook can't play dixieland jazz.

449
00:18:25,767 --> 00:18:27,867
- Alright, Joel, alright.

450
00:18:29,067 --> 00:18:30,733
- Hey, say, like
Robinson Crusoe,

451
00:18:30,767 --> 00:18:33,067
this is as primitive as can be.

452
00:18:33,100 --> 00:18:35,667
- Bet Robinson Crusoe
can't play dixieland jazz.

453
00:18:35,700 --> 00:18:38,133
- Would you knock it off
about dixieland jazz?

454
00:18:38,167 --> 00:18:40,867
- Oh, I know this, this is
the famous dock walking scene.

455
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:42,233
You know, this whole take
was done in one shot.

456
00:18:42,267 --> 00:18:45,167
It's a planned sequence, like
the opening of Touch of Evil

457
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:46,967
and the Copa Cabana
scene in Goodfellows.

458
00:18:47,067 --> 00:18:49,133
This is virtuoso
filmmaking, fellas,

459
00:18:49,167 --> 00:18:50,167
you got to see it to believe it.

460
00:18:50,200 --> 00:18:51,700
Now Brian De Palma
used this in um,

461
00:18:51,733 --> 00:18:54,200
in, in Bonfire of the Vanities,

462
00:18:54,233 --> 00:18:57,233
really exciting,
it's splendiferous.

463
00:19:02,733 --> 00:19:05,067
- Well, we've covered
the waterfront.

464
00:19:05,067 --> 00:19:06,867
- Ah, she'll ride like a duck.

465
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:07,967
If she swings too
far to starboard,

466
00:19:08,067 --> 00:19:09,633
we might have to take
a check line to shore.

467
00:19:09,667 --> 00:19:11,133
- [Tom Servo] Yeah, whatever.

468
00:19:11,167 --> 00:19:14,367
- Hey, Rook, take a
slant up that trail.

469
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:16,800
- Hey, this island's
got oak trees!

470
00:19:16,833 --> 00:19:18,833
- Now that's a rather strange
set up, wouldn't you say?

471
00:19:18,867 --> 00:19:20,500
- Yeah, but the
punchline makes it work.

472
00:19:20,533 --> 00:19:22,233
- Looks like somebody's
getting rid of somebody.

473
00:19:22,267 --> 00:19:23,067
- [Joel] Sometime.

474
00:19:23,067 --> 00:19:24,800
- Maybe.

475
00:19:24,833 --> 00:19:25,900
Hard to tell.

476
00:19:27,167 --> 00:19:29,167
- But I bet they can't
play dixieland jazz--

477
00:19:29,200 --> 00:19:30,367
- [Tom Servo] Shut up.

478
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:35,600
- Like Columbus, we claim
this island for, oh, hi.

479
00:19:40,100 --> 00:19:44,067
- [Joel] Uh, I'm the
temp you ordered.

480
00:19:44,067 --> 00:19:45,200
- I'm Dr. Craigis.

481
00:19:45,233 --> 00:19:46,567
- Yeah, right, whatever,
who's the lady?

482
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:48,133
- Got your supplies aboard.

483
00:19:48,167 --> 00:19:49,367
- That's good.

484
00:19:49,400 --> 00:19:51,467
Nothing wrong with
Captain Peterson, I hope.

485
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:52,467
- Well, I wouldn't know.

486
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:54,067
- Ever heard of Slim Fast?

487
00:19:54,067 --> 00:19:55,567
- I just bought his run.

488
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:57,167
- Oh, I see.

489
00:19:57,200 --> 00:19:58,900
Were you able to fill my order?

490
00:19:58,933 --> 00:20:00,167
- Everything on the list.

491
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:00,967
Here's the manifest.

492
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,567
- [Tom Servo] Destiny.

493
00:20:02,600 --> 00:20:06,200
- Missing last week's supply
has run us a little short.

494
00:20:06,233 --> 00:20:09,700
Captain, after you've unloaded,
I have a passenger for you.

495
00:20:09,733 --> 00:20:10,533
Ann.

496
00:20:11,767 --> 00:20:14,167
Captain Sherman, this is
my daughter Ann Craigis.

497
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:15,267
- So hands off.

498
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:18,333
- Well, so you're the passenger.

499
00:20:18,367 --> 00:20:20,633
- That's right, captain.

500
00:20:20,667 --> 00:20:23,000
- Well, it's going to be
nice having you aboard.

501
00:20:23,033 --> 00:20:24,633
I'm getting a little tired
of looking at Griswald.

502
00:20:24,667 --> 00:20:25,900
- [Joel] Can you
play dixieland jazz?

503
00:20:25,933 --> 00:20:28,633
- We're not leaving today.

504
00:20:28,667 --> 00:20:29,833
- [Joel] Oh, well, I--

505
00:20:29,867 --> 00:20:30,633
- [Tom Servo] Huh?

506
00:20:30,667 --> 00:20:31,400
Huh?

507
00:20:31,433 --> 00:20:33,000
Oh?

508
00:20:33,033 --> 00:20:35,200
- In fact, we're
not even unloading.

509
00:20:35,233 --> 00:20:36,667
- In fact, we don't even exist.

510
00:20:36,700 --> 00:20:39,833
- That load will cut
the roll in half.

511
00:20:39,867 --> 00:20:43,067
- Now wait, you don't
have a line yet.

512
00:20:43,100 --> 00:20:44,400
- Yes, animals.

513
00:20:45,567 --> 00:20:47,867
- Game or otherwise.

514
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:52,000
- Under certain conditions,
it could be dangerous.

515
00:20:52,033 --> 00:20:54,367
- [Joel] He's right, you know.

516
00:20:54,400 --> 00:20:56,733
- Hey Rook, you want
to shake the kinks out,

517
00:20:56,767 --> 00:20:58,033
limber up a little.

518
00:20:58,067 --> 00:20:59,167
- Not me, captain,
but you go right ahead

519
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:00,867
and stretch your legs.

520
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:01,967
That ship's going
to be bucking like

521
00:21:02,067 --> 00:21:03,667
a bucking bronco before long.

522
00:21:03,700 --> 00:21:05,400
'Sides, I got some
work left to do.

523
00:21:05,433 --> 00:21:07,167
- A simple yes or no will do.

524
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:09,167
- If you come
ashore, wear a gun.

525
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:10,167
- Got you, captain.

526
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:11,133
- [Tom Servo] And if
you go to San Francisco,

527
00:21:11,167 --> 00:21:13,067
wear some flowers in your hair.

528
00:21:13,067 --> 00:21:14,567
- [Joel] Gotcha, captain.

529
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:18,133
Oh, he must be scouting
locations for Dukes of Hazzard.

530
00:21:18,167 --> 00:21:18,933
- Ooh.

531
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:22,933
Boss Hogg'll love this place.

532
00:21:27,867 --> 00:21:28,633
- [Tom Servo] I'd say
they went a bit overboard

533
00:21:28,667 --> 00:21:29,900
on the fence.

534
00:21:29,933 --> 00:21:31,300
- [Joel] No sense
of perspective.

535
00:21:31,333 --> 00:21:32,067
- [Tom Servo] You know, they
should have put a gate in it,

536
00:21:32,067 --> 00:21:33,533
for crying out loud.

537
00:21:36,533 --> 00:21:39,667
- [Joel] I've fallen in with a
group of ham radio operators.

538
00:21:39,700 --> 00:21:42,067
- When I told you
about the hurricane,

539
00:21:42,067 --> 00:21:43,833
you acted like you didn't
know anything about it.

540
00:21:43,867 --> 00:21:45,167
- Hurricane Carter?

541
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:47,067
- What's the matter,
your set broken down?

542
00:21:47,067 --> 00:21:48,067
- We've been out
of communication

543
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:49,367
for more than a week, captain.

544
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:51,067
- Can it be fixed?

545
00:21:51,933 --> 00:21:54,167
- Totally out of commission.

546
00:21:54,200 --> 00:21:55,067
- What he said.

547
00:21:55,100 --> 00:21:56,733
- My assistant.

548
00:21:56,767 --> 00:21:58,400
Mr. Farrell, Jerry Farrell.

549
00:21:58,433 --> 00:21:59,900
- Jerry Farrell?

550
00:22:00,067 --> 00:22:01,867
Hey, did he go to Haroldson
Elementary School?

551
00:22:01,900 --> 00:22:04,067
- [Joel] Hey, Freddy Fender.

552
00:22:06,200 --> 00:22:09,833
- Father, perhaps the
captain will enjoy a drink.

553
00:22:09,867 --> 00:22:12,067
- Well, of course.

554
00:22:12,067 --> 00:22:13,567
Will you join us in a cocktail?

555
00:22:13,600 --> 00:22:16,067
- Well, I've never been known
to turn down a drink yet.

556
00:22:16,067 --> 00:22:17,400
I'll be glad to accept.

557
00:22:17,433 --> 00:22:19,133
- I've been known to beg for
them, as a matter of fact.

558
00:22:19,167 --> 00:22:21,067
- In that case
we'll have martinis.

559
00:22:21,067 --> 00:22:22,167
Mario.

560
00:22:22,200 --> 00:22:23,200
- Si, senor.

561
00:22:23,233 --> 00:22:25,167
- Mix them, please.

562
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:28,067
- Mix 'em please,
what's he talking about?

563
00:22:28,067 --> 00:22:29,633
- [Joel] What a scene.

564
00:22:29,667 --> 00:22:31,133
- Right this way, captain.

565
00:22:31,167 --> 00:22:32,233
- You know those
little cocktail napkins

566
00:22:32,267 --> 00:22:33,467
with the jokes on them?

567
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:35,833
I love those little guys.

568
00:22:35,867 --> 00:22:39,167
- [Tom Servo] Uh, check your
gun at the door, Festus.

569
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:41,067
- [Joel] There, bad door.

570
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:44,400
Okay, don't say hi, ga.

571
00:22:46,933 --> 00:22:49,067
- Sounds like Flash Gordon.

572
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:51,667
- [Tom Servo] What a dump.

573
00:22:51,700 --> 00:22:52,867
Who said that, George?

574
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:54,733
- You have everything
here you need?

575
00:22:54,767 --> 00:22:56,867
- We are self-sufficient here.

576
00:22:56,900 --> 00:23:00,733
We have cows for fresh milk,
chickens for fresh eggs.

577
00:23:00,767 --> 00:23:02,967
- [Tom Servo] Gin, rum,
pickled eggs, mixers.

578
00:23:03,067 --> 00:23:04,900
- Sort of a world all your own.

579
00:23:04,933 --> 00:23:06,833
- That's exactly what
I've tried to create.

580
00:23:06,867 --> 00:23:09,233
- Well, to each his own.

581
00:23:09,267 --> 00:23:10,833
If you have to be
isolated for your work,

582
00:23:10,867 --> 00:23:12,633
you've picked a
lonely little island.

583
00:23:12,667 --> 00:23:14,200
- What are you getting
at, what's your point?

584
00:23:14,233 --> 00:23:16,667
- Excuse me, please,
I think I'll change.

585
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:17,967
- Why'd you go and
say that to her?

586
00:23:18,067 --> 00:23:19,367
Geez, let's leave.

587
00:23:21,367 --> 00:23:24,200
(doors closing)

588
00:23:31,067 --> 00:23:32,667
- Tom, I knew that
shirt was going to

589
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:34,733
push some bad buttons for
Joel and get him started

590
00:23:34,767 --> 00:23:37,300
doing that Will Rogers thing
he's been talking about.

591
00:23:37,333 --> 00:23:38,633
- And you were right, Crow.

592
00:23:38,667 --> 00:23:40,400
By the way, I do love
those pants (laughs).

593
00:23:40,433 --> 00:23:42,700
- Oh, I ought to pants you.
- Shh, here he comes.

594
00:23:42,733 --> 00:23:44,900
- Well, howdy folks,
how you doing?

595
00:23:44,933 --> 00:23:46,633
You know, I've been
listening to what

596
00:23:46,667 --> 00:23:48,633
the President's
been doing, and it

597
00:23:48,667 --> 00:23:50,067
kind of seems to
me like he's trying

598
00:23:50,067 --> 00:23:53,367
to crawl into the wrong end
of the cracker barrel, hyuck.

599
00:23:53,400 --> 00:23:55,067
- Moo.

600
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:56,167
- Uh, bristle, I guess.

601
00:23:56,200 --> 00:23:57,467
- Yeah, I'm a lot
like you folks.

602
00:23:57,500 --> 00:23:59,333
I'm simple, you know,
and I been hearing

603
00:23:59,367 --> 00:24:02,200
the President go on and on,
and it kind of seems to me

604
00:24:02,233 --> 00:24:04,333
like the polecat's
got caught in the cow

605
00:24:04,367 --> 00:24:07,133
and it's time for the hound dog
to skitter him out of there.

606
00:24:07,167 --> 00:24:08,333
(Tom Servo moos)

607
00:24:08,367 --> 00:24:09,367
- Hey, this is fun.

608
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:10,300
(moos)

609
00:24:10,333 --> 00:24:11,067
- Pretty good at it, too.

610
00:24:11,067 --> 00:24:12,333
Bristle, bristle.

611
00:24:12,367 --> 00:24:13,633
- Oh yeah, you know,
kind of reminds me

612
00:24:13,667 --> 00:24:16,167
of Aunt Martha's
mustache, you know.

613
00:24:16,200 --> 00:24:19,067
Makes a fella hungry for some
down home dogfish, don't it.

614
00:24:19,067 --> 00:24:20,333
- What?

615
00:24:20,367 --> 00:24:23,133
- The rumors of my death have
been greatly exaggerated.

616
00:24:23,167 --> 00:24:24,633
- That's not Will Rogers.

617
00:24:24,667 --> 00:24:25,900
(moos)

618
00:24:25,933 --> 00:24:27,733
- Yup, it's not
over til it's over.

619
00:24:27,767 --> 00:24:29,067
- And that's Casey Stengel.

620
00:24:29,100 --> 00:24:30,133
What's he doing?

621
00:24:30,167 --> 00:24:31,067
- I don't know,
it's crazy there.

622
00:24:31,067 --> 00:24:32,067
- Yup, yup, yup.

623
00:24:32,067 --> 00:24:34,067
Oh, well everything
I love is either

624
00:24:34,067 --> 00:24:36,067
illegal, immoral, or fattening.

625
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:37,400
Take a bite out of crime.

626
00:24:37,433 --> 00:24:39,300
- Wait a minute, Alexander
Walcott and McGruff?

627
00:24:39,333 --> 00:24:40,067
He's all over the map.

628
00:24:40,100 --> 00:24:41,067
Joel, you okay?

629
00:24:41,067 --> 00:24:42,067
Come here, buddy (whistles).

630
00:24:42,067 --> 00:24:43,700
- Yup, we will bury you.

631
00:24:43,733 --> 00:24:44,733
- Khrushchev?

632
00:24:44,767 --> 00:24:46,067
Oh, Joel.

633
00:24:46,067 --> 00:24:49,333
- Yup, baseball been
berry, berry good to me.

634
00:24:49,367 --> 00:24:50,900
- Oh, now he's doing
Garrett Morris.

635
00:24:50,933 --> 00:24:51,900
- Really sad.

636
00:24:51,933 --> 00:24:52,700
- Just toying with us.

637
00:24:52,733 --> 00:24:54,067
Let's get out of here.

638
00:24:54,067 --> 00:24:56,733
- Anybody here seen
my old friend John?

639
00:24:56,767 --> 00:24:58,533
- Ah, ah, Joel, let me go!

640
00:24:58,567 --> 00:25:00,300
I want to go iron my pants!

641
00:25:00,333 --> 00:25:02,800
- Yup, yup, yup, it's
morning in America.

642
00:25:02,833 --> 00:25:04,367
- No, it is not.

643
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:05,700
- Yup.

644
00:25:05,733 --> 00:25:08,567
One fish, two fish,
red fish, blue fish.

645
00:25:08,600 --> 00:25:11,067
(light music)

646
00:25:14,833 --> 00:25:17,133
- Hey, I thought, you're
gonna hurt somebody with this.

647
00:25:17,167 --> 00:25:18,833
- [Tom Servo] Well,
it was your idea.

648
00:25:18,867 --> 00:25:20,867
- I can understand that.

649
00:25:22,567 --> 00:25:23,333
- [Joel] Ouch!

650
00:25:23,367 --> 00:25:24,233
- Sorry.

651
00:25:24,267 --> 00:25:26,233
- [Joel] How would you know?

652
00:25:26,267 --> 00:25:27,067
- Hey.

653
00:25:28,067 --> 00:25:29,100
Bob Clampett.

654
00:25:34,200 --> 00:25:36,833
- [Tom Servo] So how's your
novel coming along there?

655
00:25:36,867 --> 00:25:37,833
- Radford.

656
00:25:37,867 --> 00:25:39,667
- [Joel] Rapid?

657
00:25:39,700 --> 00:25:40,967
Bathroom?

658
00:25:41,067 --> 00:25:42,167
- Radford, would you
please step over here.

659
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:44,067
- [Joel] Rapid, bathroom?

660
00:25:44,067 --> 00:25:46,067
- Just a second, doc.

661
00:25:46,067 --> 00:25:49,067
- [Joel] Yes, this is
our resident novelist,
Rapid Bathroom.

662
00:25:49,067 --> 00:25:50,433
- Doctor Baines.

663
00:25:51,933 --> 00:25:53,967
- There's no need to shout,
doctor, I'm right here.

664
00:25:54,067 --> 00:25:56,167
- I'd like you to meet
Captain Thorne Sherman.

665
00:25:56,200 --> 00:25:58,833
- [Joel] Stone Shrimp?

666
00:25:58,867 --> 00:26:00,667
- How do you do?

667
00:26:00,700 --> 00:26:02,533
- Two new litters
since lunch, doctor.

668
00:26:02,567 --> 00:26:04,167
Both support GT-116.

669
00:26:05,067 --> 00:26:06,633
- That's good, but uh--

670
00:26:06,667 --> 00:26:09,633
- We can breed them to the 205
group in about three weeks.

671
00:26:09,667 --> 00:26:11,200
But I'd prefer to
wait and expose

672
00:26:11,233 --> 00:26:12,533
about half of them to the--

673
00:26:12,567 --> 00:26:14,667
- [Joel] Hey, relax.

674
00:26:14,700 --> 00:26:16,067
- If you agree.

675
00:26:17,400 --> 00:26:18,400
- Good idea.

676
00:26:19,900 --> 00:26:21,467
Do you know there's
a hurricane coming?

677
00:26:21,500 --> 00:26:24,067
- No, but if you hum a few bars.

678
00:26:24,067 --> 00:26:26,233
- Well I don't
suppose there's much

679
00:26:26,267 --> 00:26:28,200
we can do about that.

680
00:26:29,533 --> 00:26:31,167
Glad to meet you.

681
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:34,767
- [Joel] Could you get me a
few Denver Pyle autographs?

682
00:26:36,200 --> 00:26:38,167
- [Tom Servo] We need
the eggs, as they say.

683
00:26:38,200 --> 00:26:39,133
- You have to excuse Radford.

684
00:26:39,167 --> 00:26:40,367
- [Joel] He smells.

685
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:42,067
- Anything that does
not concern his project

686
00:26:42,067 --> 00:26:43,400
does not get through to him.

687
00:26:43,433 --> 00:26:45,400
Brilliant mind.

688
00:26:45,433 --> 00:26:46,733
He would starve to death

689
00:26:46,767 --> 00:26:48,067
if someone didn't
remind him to eat.

690
00:26:48,100 --> 00:26:49,067
- Must be very interesting work.

691
00:26:49,067 --> 00:26:50,067
What's his field?

692
00:26:50,100 --> 00:26:50,867
- [Tom Servo] Of dreams.

693
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:52,167
- Biology.

694
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:54,067
He specializes in
genetics, heredity.

695
00:26:54,067 --> 00:26:56,733
- I'm afraid my interest doesn't
run in that line, you know.

696
00:26:56,767 --> 00:26:57,567
- Get out.

697
00:26:58,700 --> 00:27:00,133
- If you could
isolate and identify

698
00:27:00,167 --> 00:27:02,067
the inherited
factor in each gene.

699
00:27:02,067 --> 00:27:03,067
- Now wait a minute, I'm afraid

700
00:27:03,067 --> 00:27:05,067
I'm not very good
with pure research.

701
00:27:05,067 --> 00:27:07,233
- This is practical research.

702
00:27:07,267 --> 00:27:08,800
- I'm not good at that, either.

703
00:27:08,833 --> 00:27:10,200
- The theory among mammals,
the smaller the size,

704
00:27:10,233 --> 00:27:13,967
the higher the metabolism
and shorter the lifespan.

705
00:27:14,067 --> 00:27:15,067
- Is that right.

706
00:27:15,067 --> 00:27:17,133
- Well, I'm attempting
to decrease the size

707
00:27:17,167 --> 00:27:19,567
by maintaining a low metabolism

708
00:27:19,600 --> 00:27:21,900
and resultant a longer lifespan.

709
00:27:21,933 --> 00:27:23,067
- What reason?

710
00:27:23,067 --> 00:27:24,633
- You think he'd come up
with a hair growth thing

711
00:27:24,667 --> 00:27:25,900
if he was so smart.

712
00:27:25,933 --> 00:27:28,733
- I'd like to hit
you really hard.

713
00:27:29,533 --> 00:27:31,167
- In time.

714
00:27:31,200 --> 00:27:33,067
- [Joel] Hey, it's Cousin
Marilyn from the Munsters.

715
00:27:33,067 --> 00:27:34,067
- Oh.

716
00:27:35,067 --> 00:27:37,067
- If we were half as
big as we were now,

717
00:27:37,067 --> 00:27:40,367
we could live twice as long
on our natural resources.

718
00:27:40,400 --> 00:27:41,633
- She's got her
apron on backwards.

719
00:27:41,667 --> 00:27:43,633
- Father, may I speak
with you a moment?

720
00:27:43,667 --> 00:27:46,300
Will you please
excuse us, captain?

721
00:27:46,333 --> 00:27:48,633
- Well, providing
you drop the captain.

722
00:27:48,667 --> 00:27:49,400
- [Tom Servo] Ouch.

723
00:27:49,433 --> 00:27:51,133
- I prefer Thorne.

724
00:27:51,167 --> 00:27:55,067
- Dad, this scientist thing is
cute, but it's wearing thin.

725
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:58,067
- [Joel] Mm hmm,
that's good booze.

726
00:28:00,367 --> 00:28:02,400
- Tell him, tell him now.

727
00:28:04,767 --> 00:28:06,067
- Tell me something, doctor.

728
00:28:06,067 --> 00:28:07,133
- [Joel] Why do
fools fall in love?

729
00:28:07,167 --> 00:28:08,967
- Has a hurricane ever
hit this island before

730
00:28:09,067 --> 00:28:10,067
that you know of?

731
00:28:10,067 --> 00:28:11,067
- I wouldn't know.

732
00:28:11,100 --> 00:28:12,867
We've only been
here nine months,

733
00:28:12,900 --> 00:28:14,900
and this place has been
unoccupied for years.

734
00:28:14,933 --> 00:28:16,467
- Well, have you ever
been through one?

735
00:28:16,500 --> 00:28:18,067
- [Tom Servo] Have
you ever been mellow?

736
00:28:18,067 --> 00:28:19,233
- Only the fringes.

737
00:28:19,267 --> 00:28:21,067
- Well, this one's
building up very fast,

738
00:28:21,067 --> 00:28:23,067
pressure dropped very
quickly in the center.

739
00:28:23,100 --> 00:28:24,533
I'd advise you to get
some doors and windows

740
00:28:24,567 --> 00:28:25,700
open in this place.

741
00:28:25,733 --> 00:28:27,600
- Yes, Mr. Ventilation.

742
00:28:29,500 --> 00:28:31,567
- Ventilators on the roof,

743
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:33,300
and if you'll freshen Ann's
drink, I'll go and check them.

744
00:28:33,333 --> 00:28:35,167
- Flesh in man's drink?

745
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:36,967
- [Tom Servo] I don't get it.

746
00:28:37,067 --> 00:28:39,333
- Thank heavens you arrived.

747
00:28:39,367 --> 00:28:40,567
- Frosty malt?

748
00:28:40,600 --> 00:28:41,833
- Why, rations
are getting short?

749
00:28:41,867 --> 00:28:44,333
- No, it's more than
that, much more.

750
00:28:44,367 --> 00:28:46,967
We'll surely be able to
sail tomorrow, won't we?

751
00:28:47,067 --> 00:28:48,167
- Possibly.

752
00:28:48,200 --> 00:28:50,167
But not probable.

753
00:28:50,200 --> 00:28:51,833
Not til late, anyway.

754
00:28:51,867 --> 00:28:54,067
Takes a long time for a sea
like that to quiet down.

755
00:28:54,100 --> 00:28:56,467
- But if you're worried
about me, you needn't.

756
00:28:56,500 --> 00:28:57,500
I'm a good sailor.

757
00:28:57,533 --> 00:29:00,100
- Aye aye, all hands on deck.

758
00:29:01,700 --> 00:29:03,133
- We'll sail when
the time's right.

759
00:29:03,167 --> 00:29:05,133
- [Marlowe] Got them
opened all right.

760
00:29:05,167 --> 00:29:07,667
- [Tom Servo] That's
great, now close them up.

761
00:29:07,700 --> 00:29:09,800
- Well captain, let
me freshen your drink.

762
00:29:09,833 --> 00:29:10,700
- [Tom Servo and Joel]
Flesh in your drink?

763
00:29:10,733 --> 00:29:12,400
- [Joel] That's what he said.

764
00:29:12,433 --> 00:29:13,700
- Not trying to rush you, but--

765
00:29:13,733 --> 00:29:15,167
- Get out, get out!

766
00:29:15,200 --> 00:29:17,800
- I've invited Thorne
for dinner, father.

767
00:29:17,833 --> 00:29:19,233
- [Joel] Does anybody
know the plot yet?

768
00:29:19,267 --> 00:29:21,067
- Oh, I see.

769
00:29:21,067 --> 00:29:23,333
- [Joel] London, I see France.

770
00:29:23,367 --> 00:29:26,633
- If you're not worried about
your ship, you're welcome.

771
00:29:26,667 --> 00:29:28,333
- I'll take a rain
check on that dinner.

772
00:29:28,367 --> 00:29:29,567
- If you wish.

773
00:29:29,600 --> 00:29:32,167
- Father, I've asked
Thorne to stay.

774
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:34,067
- Hey everybody, what
does this remind you of?

775
00:29:34,100 --> 00:29:38,067
- [Joel] He's a brilliant
man, but he saves his phlegm.

776
00:29:39,067 --> 00:29:41,067
- Sole survivor of group 30.

777
00:29:41,067 --> 00:29:43,067
28 months old today.

778
00:29:43,067 --> 00:29:47,167
* Happy birthday to you

779
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:49,467
- That's right,
yet still maintains

780
00:29:49,500 --> 00:29:52,800
low metabolism without
any sluggishness.

781
00:29:52,833 --> 00:29:54,533
- [Joel] He's the guy who
taught LBJ how to hold dogs.

782
00:29:54,567 --> 00:29:57,067
- It certainly sets our course.

783
00:29:58,167 --> 00:30:01,033
- Radford, suspend series 52--

784
00:30:01,067 --> 00:30:02,367
- [Joel] Ratford?

785
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:05,167
- What the heck is
he talking about?

786
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:06,067
- What is that?

787
00:30:06,100 --> 00:30:08,167
- It's a sorex soricidae.

788
00:30:08,200 --> 00:30:09,467
- [Tom Servo] But
I call him Rick.

789
00:30:09,500 --> 00:30:11,033
- Looks like a small rat.

790
00:30:11,067 --> 00:30:13,033
Smells like a skunk.

791
00:30:13,067 --> 00:30:15,067
- [Joel] Looks delicious.

792
00:30:15,100 --> 00:30:19,133
- These little fellas
are our subjects.

793
00:30:19,167 --> 00:30:22,633
They have a birth
cycle of 10 to 14 days,

794
00:30:22,667 --> 00:30:25,033
and using them we
can establish traits.

795
00:30:25,067 --> 00:30:27,367
- This guy makes Al
Louis look handsome.

796
00:30:27,400 --> 00:30:30,033
- Over a short period of time.

797
00:30:30,067 --> 00:30:31,633
- Well, how big do they get?

798
00:30:31,667 --> 00:30:33,033
(dramatic music)

799
00:30:33,067 --> 00:30:34,533
- How big does that
music make it sound?

800
00:30:34,567 --> 00:30:36,167
- That's an adult.

801
00:30:36,200 --> 00:30:38,533
- I've got a big, dirty secret.

802
00:30:38,567 --> 00:30:40,733
- See, the muzzle is longer
and thicker than the rats.

803
00:30:40,767 --> 00:30:42,067
- [Tom Servo] Thicker?

804
00:30:42,100 --> 00:30:43,200
- It extends over the
lower mandible, see?

805
00:30:43,233 --> 00:30:45,400
- [Joel] Guys, want to
get back to the movie?

806
00:30:45,433 --> 00:30:46,967
- [Tom Servo] Be nice.

807
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:48,500
- I can't think of my line.

808
00:30:48,533 --> 00:30:52,000
- All he knows is his next meal.

809
00:30:52,033 --> 00:30:54,033
- [Joel] Which is you.

810
00:30:54,067 --> 00:30:56,167
- He's just been fed.

811
00:30:56,200 --> 00:30:57,967
He'll be alright for
another eight hours.

812
00:30:58,000 --> 00:30:59,133
(loud banging)

813
00:30:59,167 --> 00:31:00,133
(Ann screams)

814
00:31:00,167 --> 00:31:01,733
- Honey, switch to decaf.

815
00:31:01,767 --> 00:31:02,967
- A shutter blew open.

816
00:31:03,067 --> 00:31:04,067
Radford.

817
00:31:04,100 --> 00:31:06,533
- I've got it.
- Ow, aw, gee.

818
00:31:07,567 --> 00:31:09,067
- You let that scare you?

819
00:31:09,067 --> 00:31:10,700
- [Tom Servo] Does it bug you?

820
00:31:10,733 --> 00:31:12,067
- Storms have always
frightened her,

821
00:31:12,100 --> 00:31:14,200
ever since she was a child.

822
00:31:15,733 --> 00:31:19,933
(singing tune from
Hawaii Five-O)

823
00:31:24,067 --> 00:31:26,067
They're not climbers.

824
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:29,067
They are digging animals,
a lot like the mole.

825
00:31:29,067 --> 00:31:30,367
- Let me drop it
down your shirt.

826
00:31:30,400 --> 00:31:33,200
- They feed only at night
unless they're starving.

827
00:31:33,233 --> 00:31:35,067
And when they are hungry enough,

828
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:38,167
they'll tackle anything,
regardless of size.

829
00:31:38,200 --> 00:31:39,967
- [Joel] Like LT,
Lawrence Taylor.

830
00:31:40,067 --> 00:31:42,900
- Keep two of them in a cage
for 12 hours without food,

831
00:31:42,933 --> 00:31:45,067
the stronger will
eat the weaker.

832
00:31:45,067 --> 00:31:45,967
- It's fun.

833
00:31:46,067 --> 00:31:48,133
- They're cannibals.
- Precisely.

834
00:31:48,167 --> 00:31:50,133
- [Joel] Don't say cannibal.

835
00:31:50,167 --> 00:31:53,367
- Requires a tremendous
amount of energy.

836
00:31:53,400 --> 00:31:57,067
To supply it, they
must eat three times

837
00:31:57,067 --> 00:32:01,233
their own weight in food
every 24 hours, or starve.

838
00:32:01,267 --> 00:32:03,067
- Guys, guys, that was
covered in the voiceover

839
00:32:03,067 --> 00:32:05,433
at the beginning of the movie.

840
00:32:07,067 --> 00:32:08,733
- [Joel] Alright, I want you
to pick a card, any card,

841
00:32:08,767 --> 00:32:13,067
out of the middle of
the deck is fine now.

842
00:32:13,100 --> 00:32:15,167
- Some call them bone eaters.

843
00:32:15,200 --> 00:32:16,700
- I call them cheddarwurst.

844
00:32:16,733 --> 00:32:18,733
- When the flesh is
gone, they'll eat
the bones for marrow.

845
00:32:18,767 --> 00:32:21,900
All they'll leave are
teeth, horns, hooves--

846
00:32:21,933 --> 00:32:24,167
- I could use another
martini, do you mind?

847
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:25,667
- Of course, my dear.

848
00:32:25,700 --> 00:32:27,067
I'll get you one.

849
00:32:28,333 --> 00:32:31,467
- You don't pick
your family, do you?

850
00:32:31,500 --> 00:32:34,500
- Just humor him,
he's not well, hi.

851
00:32:35,367 --> 00:32:36,767
- There you are.

852
00:32:37,900 --> 00:32:38,967
- [Tom Servo] Drink it down.

853
00:32:39,067 --> 00:32:40,400
- Mario.

854
00:32:40,433 --> 00:32:41,633
Mario!

855
00:32:41,667 --> 00:32:43,067
- Yes, Mr. Beatty.

856
00:32:44,900 --> 00:32:46,467
- Mario will show
you to the bath,

857
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:48,067
you will freshen up a bit.

858
00:32:48,067 --> 00:32:52,200
(speaking in foreign language)

859
00:32:54,167 --> 00:32:56,333
- Hit him with the bathroom?

860
00:32:59,067 --> 00:33:02,200
- Oh Nita, what are
you trying to prove?

861
00:33:02,233 --> 00:33:04,567
- [Joel] Finish your drink.

862
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:10,067
Well, listen, here's
the caveman room.

863
00:33:10,067 --> 00:33:12,200
You'll really enjoy it.

864
00:33:12,233 --> 00:33:13,833
- Turtle Bulkowski
was here before you,

865
00:33:13,867 --> 00:33:15,200
sorry about the mess.

866
00:33:15,233 --> 00:33:18,900
- The martini faucet's in
back, oh, you found it.

867
00:33:18,933 --> 00:33:21,833
- Oh, strip over the toilet,
sanitized for my protection.

868
00:33:21,867 --> 00:33:24,967
- Is your room comfortable, sir?

869
00:33:25,067 --> 00:33:27,233
Can I unpack your hat?

870
00:33:27,267 --> 00:33:31,833
- [Joel] Uh, please don't
drink from the bidet.

871
00:33:31,867 --> 00:33:33,833
- You got a problem, too?

872
00:33:33,867 --> 00:33:35,067
Que paso?

873
00:33:35,067 --> 00:33:37,233
- Nada, nothing, senor.

874
00:33:37,267 --> 00:33:40,367
But you will leave
on the ship tomorrow.

875
00:33:40,400 --> 00:33:41,733
- Well, I think that all depends

876
00:33:41,767 --> 00:33:43,833
on the wind and
the sea, don't you?

877
00:33:43,867 --> 00:33:45,133
- Si.

878
00:33:45,167 --> 00:33:47,333
- [Joel] Regular Walt
Whitman, aren't you?

879
00:33:47,367 --> 00:33:48,700
- I don't know.

880
00:33:49,867 --> 00:33:52,867
Ah, you're joking.
(laughing)

881
00:33:52,900 --> 00:33:53,700
I see it, boy.

882
00:33:53,733 --> 00:33:55,167
I see.

883
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:57,500
- Well, I'm glad to hear that.

884
00:33:57,533 --> 00:33:59,867
If anybody else is concerned
about my sailing tomorrow

885
00:33:59,900 --> 00:34:02,233
why, you see they feel
the point too, huh?

886
00:34:02,267 --> 00:34:03,200
- Ouch.
- I don't like

887
00:34:03,233 --> 00:34:04,733
repeating myself.

888
00:34:04,767 --> 00:34:05,967
- Okay, Senor.

889
00:34:06,067 --> 00:34:08,433
(upbeat music)

890
00:34:11,400 --> 00:34:13,833
(whooshing)

891
00:34:15,767 --> 00:34:19,733
- [Joel] Oh, he's got to plug
in the boat for the night.

892
00:34:19,767 --> 00:34:23,167
Aw, someone left the
cake out in the rain.

893
00:34:25,267 --> 00:34:28,267
- [Tom Servo] What's
he doing there?

894
00:34:29,433 --> 00:34:30,467
Oh, I get it!

895
00:34:30,500 --> 00:34:32,400
This is Knot's Landing!

896
00:34:36,100 --> 00:34:39,967
- I know, but I'm not saying
that you created them.

897
00:34:40,067 --> 00:34:42,700
I am saying that because
of your drunken stupidity

898
00:34:42,733 --> 00:34:44,233
in leaving the cage door open,

899
00:34:44,267 --> 00:34:47,333
you created the horrible
situation that now exists.

900
00:34:47,367 --> 00:34:48,633
- [Tom Servo] Now, Miss Kitty--

901
00:34:48,667 --> 00:34:52,067
- Like any one of
us might have made.

902
00:34:52,100 --> 00:34:53,967
I'm getting a little
sick of being called

903
00:34:54,067 --> 00:34:56,700
an irresponsible drunk.
(Joel coughing)

904
00:34:56,733 --> 00:34:59,067
Now believe me, I am.

905
00:34:59,100 --> 00:35:00,367
- [Tom Servo] Hi, is
the irresponsible drunk,

906
00:35:00,400 --> 00:35:02,333
oh, there you are!

907
00:35:02,367 --> 00:35:03,533
- Excuse me.

908
00:35:03,567 --> 00:35:07,200
- [Tom Servo] Two people
per scene, please.

909
00:35:07,233 --> 00:35:08,867
- [Joel] All right, I'm leaving,

910
00:35:08,900 --> 00:35:11,867
and as soon as I close the
door, I'm gone, all right?

911
00:35:11,900 --> 00:35:12,700
So, shrew?

912
00:35:15,400 --> 00:35:16,633
- Care for a cigarette?

913
00:35:16,667 --> 00:35:17,467
- [Tom Servo] Why, thank you.

914
00:35:17,500 --> 00:35:18,733
I have emphysema.

915
00:35:18,767 --> 00:35:19,733
- Thank heavens
you arrived today.

916
00:35:19,767 --> 00:35:20,667
- You said that before.

917
00:35:20,700 --> 00:35:22,133
What's the strain?

918
00:35:22,167 --> 00:35:23,500
- [Ann] I don't understand.

919
00:35:23,533 --> 00:35:28,067
- [Tom Servo] Aw, nuts, I got
my shirt caught in the door.

920
00:35:28,067 --> 00:35:30,967
- I want to leave, but I
want my father to go with me.

921
00:35:31,067 --> 00:35:33,167
- Well then, why doesn't he?

922
00:35:35,433 --> 00:35:37,800
- We've discussed it many
times, but he's adamant.

923
00:35:37,833 --> 00:35:38,700
- [Tom Servo] Oh, he's Adam Ant.

924
00:35:38,733 --> 00:35:40,067
I didn't know.

925
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:42,067
- I'm sure Jerry has a lot
to do with his decision.

926
00:35:42,067 --> 00:35:43,233
- [Tom Servo] Now
who's this Jerry the,

927
00:35:43,267 --> 00:35:44,900
oh, I'm Jerry.

928
00:35:44,933 --> 00:35:46,800
- Had to convince me that
the crisis will only last

929
00:35:46,833 --> 00:35:48,400
for a few--
- Oh, hi.

930
00:35:49,667 --> 00:35:51,167
- [Joel] What?

931
00:35:51,200 --> 00:35:52,233
Oh, you see, I forgot my thing,

932
00:35:52,267 --> 00:35:54,567
and, huh, I need it for
this thing I'm doing.

933
00:35:54,600 --> 00:35:56,667
- This is certainly
a cozy little scene.

934
00:35:56,700 --> 00:35:58,667
- [Tom Servo] It
was gonna be cozy.

935
00:35:58,700 --> 00:36:00,067
- Might even be called intimate.

936
00:36:00,067 --> 00:36:02,567
Boy meets girl--
- Stop it, Jer.

937
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:06,400
What I do or have to say
does not concern you.

938
00:36:07,533 --> 00:36:09,533
- I can't disagree
with you more, my dear.

939
00:36:09,567 --> 00:36:11,067
I'm concerned with
everything you do

940
00:36:11,067 --> 00:36:12,067
and I intend to
keep it that way.

941
00:36:12,100 --> 00:36:13,400
- [Joel] I'm outta here.

942
00:36:13,433 --> 00:36:15,733
- Now, in my book an engagement
isn't a casual thing.

943
00:36:15,767 --> 00:36:17,367
- In my book it isn't either.

944
00:36:17,400 --> 00:36:19,633
And last night ended ours.

945
00:36:19,667 --> 00:36:22,067
(wind whooshing)

946
00:36:22,100 --> 00:36:24,867
- [Tom Servo] Dorothy, Dorothy!

947
00:36:28,400 --> 00:36:31,133
- Hey, that sounds like
a Dixieland jazz band.

948
00:36:31,167 --> 00:36:33,067
- Now he's doing it.
- Oh, stop it!

949
00:36:33,067 --> 00:36:36,133
(wind whooshing)
- Careful.

950
00:36:36,167 --> 00:36:37,367
Cute little gun.

951
00:36:37,400 --> 00:36:38,333
Puppies!

952
00:36:38,367 --> 00:36:41,833
- Oh, yeah.
(gunshots)

953
00:36:41,867 --> 00:36:42,867
- [Tom Servo] Sit, stay!

954
00:36:42,900 --> 00:36:43,800
Sit, oh, you stay!

955
00:36:43,833 --> 00:36:45,633
You stay, you stay, you stay!

956
00:36:45,667 --> 00:36:50,167
Sit! (smooching)
(thunder rumbling)

957
00:36:50,200 --> 00:36:51,533
- [Tom Servo] Oh, will ya
look at those pants go?

958
00:36:51,567 --> 00:36:54,167
- Kinda feels good, mmm.

959
00:36:54,200 --> 00:36:56,367
(suspenseful music)
- Uh-oh.

960
00:36:56,400 --> 00:36:57,567
- [Tom Servo] Uh-oh, big dog!

961
00:36:57,600 --> 00:37:00,200
Oh, run! (sputtering)

962
00:37:00,233 --> 00:37:03,167
(thunder rumbling)
(suspenseful music)

963
00:37:03,200 --> 00:37:03,967
Hmm.

964
00:37:04,067 --> 00:37:07,067
(Tom Servo babbling)

965
00:37:08,267 --> 00:37:09,567
- Gee, they're just puppies.

966
00:37:09,600 --> 00:37:11,367
What's he running
from puppies for?

967
00:37:11,400 --> 00:37:12,967
- [Joel] Hi there,
I'm Kurt Dowdy.

968
00:37:13,067 --> 00:37:15,067
We're out hunting with George
Kirby from Big Texas Shrew

969
00:37:15,067 --> 00:37:18,067
and it's really not
going that well.

970
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:21,067
- [Tom Servo] Ren, Stimpy, no!

971
00:37:21,100 --> 00:37:21,867
- Help, help!

972
00:37:21,900 --> 00:37:23,067
Captain Thorne!

973
00:37:23,100 --> 00:37:23,867
Somebody help!
- Go away, go away!

974
00:37:23,900 --> 00:37:25,633
I'll help you.

975
00:37:25,667 --> 00:37:27,533
- [Tom Servo] Hey, just
throw us your wallet.

976
00:37:27,567 --> 00:37:30,533
(thunder rumbling)

977
00:37:30,567 --> 00:37:33,067
Wait 'til I finish my Saratoga.

978
00:37:34,267 --> 00:37:35,833
- Be reasonable.

979
00:37:35,867 --> 00:37:38,133
Everybody in the world is
scared to death of something.

980
00:37:38,167 --> 00:37:39,300
- That's evident.

981
00:37:39,333 --> 00:37:40,833
When they came at us last night,

982
00:37:40,867 --> 00:37:42,967
you knocked me down
getting inside the fence.

983
00:37:43,067 --> 00:37:44,567
- It's just that I
can't stand them.

984
00:37:44,600 --> 00:37:46,867
- Oh, now I've discovered
something I can't stand.

985
00:37:46,900 --> 00:37:47,800
- Spackle.

986
00:37:47,833 --> 00:37:48,567
It bugs me.

987
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:49,867
- All right.

988
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:52,167
But I advise you not to
mention it to anyone.

989
00:37:52,200 --> 00:37:53,533
- [Tom Servo] And don't
leave the country.

990
00:37:53,567 --> 00:37:55,067
- I'm gonna complete these
experiments with your father

991
00:37:55,100 --> 00:37:57,167
regardless of anything,
do you understand that?

992
00:37:57,200 --> 00:37:59,167
Nobody is gonna take
this chance away from me.

993
00:37:59,200 --> 00:38:02,900
- [Tom Servo] Your
breath smells like feet!

994
00:38:03,733 --> 00:38:05,500
- Where's Captain Sherman?

995
00:38:05,533 --> 00:38:06,400
- Checking the weather.

996
00:38:06,433 --> 00:38:08,067
I'll get him.

997
00:38:08,100 --> 00:38:11,067
(Joel humming)

998
00:38:11,067 --> 00:38:12,400
(exclaiming)

999
00:38:12,433 --> 00:38:13,900
- [Ann] Captain,
time for dinner.

1000
00:38:13,933 --> 00:38:15,067
- [Joel] Man, it's
really breezy in my room.

1001
00:38:15,067 --> 00:38:16,133
- [Tom Servo] We're
having tobacco salad

1002
00:38:16,167 --> 00:38:19,067
with a light gin dressing.

1003
00:38:19,067 --> 00:38:20,700
- Meanwhile, back at the branch.

1004
00:38:20,733 --> 00:38:21,500
- [Tom Servo] D'oh!

1005
00:38:21,533 --> 00:38:22,700
- Help!

1006
00:38:22,733 --> 00:38:23,300
Somebody, help!
- Hang in there, baby.

1007
00:38:23,333 --> 00:38:24,533
Friday's coming.

1008
00:38:25,500 --> 00:38:28,167
(shrews screeching)
- Ooh.

1009
00:38:28,200 --> 00:38:30,167
You know, this is another
one of those scenes

1010
00:38:30,200 --> 00:38:33,133
that is so offensive
on so many levels.

1011
00:38:33,167 --> 00:38:34,400
- [Tom Servo] I hear ya.

1012
00:38:34,433 --> 00:38:36,567
- [Tom Servo] Uh-oh,
he's shooting the ground!

1013
00:38:36,600 --> 00:38:40,667
- [Tom Servo] A little
Roscoe for ya, shew!

1014
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:42,067
(Rook screaming)

1015
00:38:42,067 --> 00:38:43,567
- D'oh, they're
licking me, stop it!

1016
00:38:43,600 --> 00:38:45,800
No, stop it, cut
it out! (laughing)

1017
00:38:45,833 --> 00:38:47,133
Oh, it tickles!

1018
00:38:47,167 --> 00:38:48,067
- [Tom Servo] They're
licking him to death.

1019
00:38:48,067 --> 00:38:50,300
How cute! (laughing)

1020
00:38:50,333 --> 00:38:52,133
(shrews screeching)

1021
00:38:52,167 --> 00:38:54,433
- I'm shakin' the bush, boss.

1022
00:38:55,900 --> 00:38:57,733
(suspenseful music)
(shrews screeching)

1023
00:38:57,767 --> 00:38:58,667
- Hmm.
(thunder rumbling)

1024
00:38:58,700 --> 00:39:00,233
- [Joel] Ooh, he exploded!

1025
00:39:00,267 --> 00:39:01,533
Hmm.

1026
00:39:01,567 --> 00:39:03,567
- I hate the smell of wet shrew.

1027
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:05,067
(rumbling)

1028
00:39:05,067 --> 00:39:06,633
- [Tom Servo] Are the
dogs and shrews friends?

1029
00:39:06,667 --> 00:39:09,067
Is that what's going on?

1030
00:39:09,100 --> 00:39:10,400
(Ann gasping)
(explosion)

1031
00:39:10,433 --> 00:39:11,633
- [Joel] I think I just
heard Griswald explode.

1032
00:39:11,667 --> 00:39:12,400
You?
- A miss is as good as a mile,

1033
00:39:12,433 --> 00:39:13,967
I always say.

1034
00:39:14,067 --> 00:39:15,167
- You know, if one of those
trees crashes through,

1035
00:39:15,200 --> 00:39:16,800
it might just knock the whole--

1036
00:39:16,833 --> 00:39:18,067
- Look, honey, any tree
on this side of the house

1037
00:39:18,100 --> 00:39:19,300
will fall away from
it, so just relax.

1038
00:39:19,333 --> 00:39:20,867
- [Tom Servo] Oh, you're
a tree surgeon now?

1039
00:39:20,900 --> 00:39:23,300
- I think I'd better
go back aboard.

1040
00:39:23,333 --> 00:39:25,300
Oh, don't think I'm not
grateful for your hospitality.

1041
00:39:25,333 --> 00:39:27,367
I am.
- But you're out of liquor so.

1042
00:39:27,400 --> 00:39:29,667
- Your ship is safe.

1043
00:39:29,700 --> 00:39:32,333
Please, stay here with me.

1044
00:39:32,367 --> 00:39:33,967
- [Joel] Is there
somebody under that dress?

1045
00:39:34,067 --> 00:39:35,733
- Are you scared or lonesome?

1046
00:39:35,767 --> 00:39:38,067
- [Tom Servo] Chicken,
chicken. (bawking)

1047
00:39:38,067 --> 00:39:39,467
- I'll take a rain check on it.

1048
00:39:39,500 --> 00:39:42,067
- Thorne, you can't leave.

1049
00:39:42,100 --> 00:39:45,467
No one opens that
gate after dark.

1050
00:39:45,500 --> 00:39:48,133
- Well, who's
gonna stop me, you?

1051
00:39:48,167 --> 00:39:49,467
- How about this?

1052
00:39:49,500 --> 00:39:50,667
- [Joel] How about
my friend, Roscoe?

1053
00:39:50,700 --> 00:39:51,867
- No one opens
that gate at night.

1054
00:39:51,900 --> 00:39:54,400
(thunder rumbling)

1055
00:39:54,433 --> 00:39:55,833
- [Tom Servo] Open that
gate, come on, Robin!

1056
00:39:55,867 --> 00:39:56,967
Open that gate!

1057
00:39:57,067 --> 00:39:58,167
- Now, look, I
don't ask questions

1058
00:39:58,200 --> 00:39:59,633
because it's against
my principles.

1059
00:39:59,667 --> 00:40:01,000
- [Tom Servo] Your what?

1060
00:40:01,033 --> 00:40:02,867
- Wouldn't you like
to explain that?

1061
00:40:02,900 --> 00:40:05,000
- [Joel] Well, you see,
the hammer hits the primer

1062
00:40:05,033 --> 00:40:06,700
and the primer--
- Give me the gun.

1063
00:40:06,733 --> 00:40:09,333
Not very becoming anyway.
- Oh, rats.

1064
00:40:09,367 --> 00:40:10,700
- You believe in fairy tales?

1065
00:40:10,733 --> 00:40:12,367
- [Tom Servo] Does
it got elves in it?

1066
00:40:12,400 --> 00:40:14,233
- Well, I'm a little old
for that sort of thing,

1067
00:40:14,267 --> 00:40:16,000
but what'd you have in mind?

1068
00:40:16,033 --> 00:40:18,033
- Well, I'll tell you about one.

1069
00:40:18,067 --> 00:40:20,867
A true fairy tale, and you
arrived in the middle of it.

1070
00:40:20,900 --> 00:40:21,700
- Oh?

1071
00:40:22,867 --> 00:40:24,167
- [Ann] Have you ever
heard of a shrew?

1072
00:40:24,200 --> 00:40:27,000
- As in Taming of the--
- No, the animal.

1073
00:40:27,033 --> 00:40:29,333
Radford called them
sorex soricidae when
he showed you one.

1074
00:40:29,367 --> 00:40:32,133
- Oh, then shrew must
be the common name

1075
00:40:32,167 --> 00:40:33,500
for those cute little animals.

1076
00:40:33,533 --> 00:40:34,800
- Cute?
- No, they're adorable.

1077
00:40:34,833 --> 00:40:35,700
- That's the last
word you can use

1078
00:40:35,733 --> 00:40:37,700
to describe those monsters.

1079
00:40:37,733 --> 00:40:40,900
They're the most
horrible animals on
the face of the earth.

1080
00:40:40,933 --> 00:40:41,700
- A murderer?

1081
00:40:41,733 --> 00:40:42,967
- My father told you,

1082
00:40:43,000 --> 00:40:44,033
they breed within three
weeks after birth.

1083
00:40:44,067 --> 00:40:45,533
Their lifespan is
around one year.

1084
00:40:45,567 --> 00:40:47,000
- Yeah, I know what
your father told me,

1085
00:40:47,033 --> 00:40:50,000
but what's that got to do
with me opening that gate?

1086
00:40:50,033 --> 00:40:51,300
- Pick another category, please.

1087
00:40:51,333 --> 00:40:52,633
Still your turn.

1088
00:40:52,667 --> 00:40:55,233
- There are 200 or 300
giant shrews out there.

1089
00:40:55,267 --> 00:40:56,833
- [Joel] Not counting
the dogs, right?

1090
00:40:56,867 --> 00:40:59,500
- Monsters weighing
between 50 and 100 pounds.

1091
00:40:59,533 --> 00:41:01,867
- [Tom Servo] How
did she weigh 'em?

1092
00:41:01,900 --> 00:41:04,133
- 50 to 100 pounds?
- Pounds?

1093
00:41:04,167 --> 00:41:06,067
- Wait a minute,
you must be kidding.

1094
00:41:06,100 --> 00:41:08,300
- I am definitely not kidding.

1095
00:41:08,333 --> 00:41:10,067
- [Tom Servo] I'm
not pulling your leg.

1096
00:41:10,067 --> 00:41:11,633
- [Thorne] That's as big
as a full-grown wolf.

1097
00:41:11,667 --> 00:41:15,067
- And what's more, they
are beginning to starve.

1098
00:41:15,100 --> 00:41:19,133
- [Joel] She's got a real
Hawaii 5-0 hairdo there.

1099
00:41:19,167 --> 00:41:21,067
- No wonder you didn't
want me to go out there.

1100
00:41:21,067 --> 00:41:23,700
Thanks for saving my skin.

1101
00:41:23,733 --> 00:41:26,533
- Well, I'm sorry I had to
threaten you with a gun.

1102
00:41:26,567 --> 00:41:28,200
But I didn't know
how else to stop you.

1103
00:41:28,233 --> 00:41:30,500
- No, it was very effective.
- Quite a turn-on.

1104
00:41:30,533 --> 00:41:32,500
- All you had to do
was tell me about--

1105
00:41:32,533 --> 00:41:34,367
- What is this,
fill in the blank?

1106
00:41:34,400 --> 00:41:37,533
- But you changed everything
when you started to leave,

1107
00:41:37,567 --> 00:41:39,400
and I had to stop you some way.

1108
00:41:39,433 --> 00:41:41,133
- [Joel] I would've been alone.

1109
00:41:41,167 --> 00:41:42,133
I'd rather kill
you than be alone.

1110
00:41:42,167 --> 00:41:43,800
- You say there's
200 or 300 out there?

1111
00:41:43,833 --> 00:41:44,833
- [Tom Servo] Where'd
they get our address?

1112
00:41:44,867 --> 00:41:45,667
- Ann.

1113
00:41:47,267 --> 00:41:50,967
- [Tom Servo] Well,
now, Matthew, I sure
could use a gimlet.

1114
00:41:51,067 --> 00:41:52,967
- That's right, Captain Thorne.

1115
00:41:53,067 --> 00:41:55,067
And if you'd stuck with your
rowboat and played captain

1116
00:41:55,100 --> 00:41:57,167
instead of trying
to play detective,

1117
00:41:57,200 --> 00:41:58,233
you wouldn't have to worry about

1118
00:41:58,267 --> 00:41:59,700
how many are out
there, would you?

1119
00:41:59,733 --> 00:42:01,067
- [Dr. Craigis]
That's enough, Jerry.

1120
00:42:01,100 --> 00:42:02,400
- That's a pretty complex
insult for a drunk.

1121
00:42:02,433 --> 00:42:03,867
- [Joel] Mm-hmm.

1122
00:42:05,067 --> 00:42:07,333
Oh, now it's like an
Agatha Christie novel, hmm?

1123
00:42:07,367 --> 00:42:08,533
- [Dr. Craigis]
What's wrong, Ann?

1124
00:42:08,567 --> 00:42:10,300
- Mr. Thorne decided to leave.

1125
00:42:10,333 --> 00:42:13,367
To dissuade him I started to
tell him about the shrews.

1126
00:42:13,400 --> 00:42:15,733
- [Tom Servo] Shoes, what
about, oh, shrews, oh!

1127
00:42:15,767 --> 00:42:16,700
- [Dr. Craigis] You might
as well know the whole story

1128
00:42:16,733 --> 00:42:18,067
as half of it.
- For story?

1129
00:42:18,067 --> 00:42:20,967
- [Joel] Doesn't anybody
think about Griswald?

1130
00:42:21,067 --> 00:42:23,400
- [Tom Servo] I've
heard this one before.

1131
00:42:23,433 --> 00:42:25,133
- [Dr. Craigis] I guess we're
all a little jumpy, Captain.

1132
00:42:25,167 --> 00:42:26,867
- Yeah, I'm sorry.

1133
00:42:26,900 --> 00:42:29,167
- [Joel] You're about to
light the filter, Captain.

1134
00:42:29,200 --> 00:42:31,067
- Six months ago we
managed to isolate

1135
00:42:31,100 --> 00:42:32,967
the factor controlling size.

1136
00:42:33,067 --> 00:42:33,967
- [Joel] Ah, what
a night that was.

1137
00:42:34,067 --> 00:42:35,333
- Two litters were born.

1138
00:42:35,367 --> 00:42:37,533
Six individuals kept for study.

1139
00:42:37,567 --> 00:42:39,133
- [Joel] A prosthetic?

1140
00:42:39,167 --> 00:42:41,300
- They were about the
size of buckshot at birth.

1141
00:42:41,333 --> 00:42:43,200
- Hey, he's tall.
- But the rate of growth

1142
00:42:43,233 --> 00:42:46,967
was abnormal.
- I'm on the edge of my chair.

1143
00:42:47,067 --> 00:42:48,200
- They continued to grow, grow.

1144
00:42:48,233 --> 00:42:50,533
- Whoa.
- They were mutants.

1145
00:42:52,067 --> 00:42:53,867
- Jailbreak tonight!

1146
00:42:54,733 --> 00:42:57,067
Everyone through the wire!

1147
00:42:57,100 --> 00:43:01,067
- [Dr. Craigis] Somehow
they managed to escape.

1148
00:43:02,933 --> 00:43:05,433
(upbeat music)

1149
00:43:09,067 --> 00:43:10,067
- One, two.

1150
00:43:10,067 --> 00:43:11,500
- One, two, three, four.

1151
00:43:11,533 --> 00:43:12,833
* Killer shrew

1152
00:43:12,867 --> 00:43:14,067
* Killer shrew

1153
00:43:14,100 --> 00:43:16,167
* Don't know the diff
between me and you

1154
00:43:16,200 --> 00:43:17,067
* It comes on at night

1155
00:43:17,100 --> 00:43:18,400
* To give you a fright

1156
00:43:18,433 --> 00:43:20,900
* Don't look now but
he's gonna take a bite

1157
00:43:20,933 --> 00:43:21,900
* Da da da da

1158
00:43:21,933 --> 00:43:22,967
* Da da da da da da-da-da

1159
00:43:23,067 --> 00:43:24,167
* Killer shrew

1160
00:43:24,200 --> 00:43:25,067
* Killer shrew

1161
00:43:25,067 --> 00:43:27,367
* K-I-Double L-E-R shrew

1162
00:43:27,400 --> 00:43:28,633
* He's scary and tall

1163
00:43:28,667 --> 00:43:29,867
* If that's not enough

1164
00:43:29,900 --> 00:43:32,367
* He's augmented with
bad man and stuff

1165
00:43:32,400 --> 00:43:33,233
* Killer shrew

1166
00:43:33,267 --> 00:43:34,533
* Killer shrew

1167
00:43:34,567 --> 00:43:36,733
* He's coming to
your town to get you

1168
00:43:36,767 --> 00:43:37,967
- Killer shrew, by Marx.

1169
00:43:38,067 --> 00:43:39,067
- All right!

1170
00:43:39,100 --> 00:43:40,867
That was great!

1171
00:43:40,900 --> 00:43:42,500
- Okay, well, consumer Joel.

1172
00:43:42,533 --> 00:43:44,067
That's the enticing jingle.

1173
00:43:44,067 --> 00:43:45,367
Now play the
super-fun board game!

1174
00:43:45,400 --> 00:43:47,067
- Wait a minute, you
guys came up with this

1175
00:43:47,100 --> 00:43:48,067
all by yourselves?

1176
00:43:48,067 --> 00:43:49,667
- El posivito, senorito.

1177
00:43:49,700 --> 00:43:51,067
C'mon, let's play.

1178
00:43:51,067 --> 00:43:52,400
You can be the German guy
and I'll pick the card.

1179
00:43:52,433 --> 00:43:53,333
Right over there.

1180
00:43:53,367 --> 00:43:55,133
- All right, okay, gotcha.

1181
00:43:55,167 --> 00:43:58,367
It says, "Contemplate shrews."

1182
00:43:58,400 --> 00:44:00,200
- Okay, that means
you don't move.

1183
00:44:00,233 --> 00:44:01,800
Okay, now I pick one.

1184
00:44:01,833 --> 00:44:03,367
- Okay, I'll do that for you.

1185
00:44:03,400 --> 00:44:04,500
Okay.

1186
00:44:04,533 --> 00:44:06,400
- Okay, have another martini.

1187
00:44:06,433 --> 00:44:10,167
Okey-doke, I'm the girl, so
I'll just move one piece.

1188
00:44:10,200 --> 00:44:13,400
Oh, you see, it's glued
down, and you can't move it.

1189
00:44:13,433 --> 00:44:15,300
It's just like the
movie, nobody moves!

1190
00:44:15,333 --> 00:44:17,200
(Crow and Tom Servo laughing)

1191
00:44:17,233 --> 00:44:20,067
In fact, nobody moves at
all for over 45 minutes!

1192
00:44:20,100 --> 00:44:22,167
It's enough to numb your brain.

1193
00:44:22,200 --> 00:44:24,167
They sit and talk
and nothing happens,

1194
00:44:24,200 --> 00:44:27,067
and then, cripes, Frank Cannon
gets more action than this.

1195
00:44:27,100 --> 00:44:27,967
- Crow--

1196
00:44:28,067 --> 00:44:29,233
- You think something
might happen

1197
00:44:29,267 --> 00:44:30,833
and your spirit soars,
but all that happens

1198
00:44:30,867 --> 00:44:32,067
is the black girl changes
and she's not even that cute!

1199
00:44:32,067 --> 00:44:33,067
- Crow--

1200
00:44:33,067 --> 00:44:34,633
- And the Cuban guy mumbles
- Crow--

1201
00:44:34,667 --> 00:44:36,067
- and so does the Captain
and they all just drink more!

1202
00:44:36,100 --> 00:44:39,233
- I'm scared, I'm scared!
(Crow babbling)

1203
00:44:39,267 --> 00:44:41,567
- Can't tell what this is!

1204
00:44:41,600 --> 00:44:43,133
(Crow and Tom Servo sobbing)

1205
00:44:43,167 --> 00:44:45,967
- Aww, that's okay, that's okay.

1206
00:44:46,067 --> 00:44:50,067
Joel's got you, it's
all right, it's okay.

1207
00:44:50,100 --> 00:44:53,300
No, you guys, you didn't
have to do this for me.

1208
00:44:53,333 --> 00:44:55,133
You're both such troopers,

1209
00:44:55,167 --> 00:44:58,133
in the theater, quippin'
like there's no tomorrow,

1210
00:44:58,167 --> 00:45:02,500
making kooky comments
on contemporary mores.

1211
00:45:02,533 --> 00:45:04,300
You know, making
arcane references

1212
00:45:04,333 --> 00:45:07,867
at the drop of a hat,
I love you little guys!

1213
00:45:07,900 --> 00:45:08,900
- Thanks, Joel.

1214
00:45:08,933 --> 00:45:10,200
We love you too.

1215
00:45:10,233 --> 00:45:12,367
- It's just, you were
so funny in the theater,

1216
00:45:12,400 --> 00:45:16,067
we didn't know if you could
tell this movie was a honk shoe.

1217
00:45:16,067 --> 00:45:17,067
- A honk shoe?

1218
00:45:17,100 --> 00:45:18,367
What's that?

1219
00:45:18,400 --> 00:45:20,400
- Oh, you know, honk
shoe. (imitating snoring)

1220
00:45:20,433 --> 00:45:23,467
(all laughing)

1221
00:45:23,500 --> 00:45:25,067
(all sighing)

1222
00:45:25,067 --> 00:45:26,833
(beeping)
(all exclaiming)

1223
00:45:26,867 --> 00:45:30,067
(clicking, whooshing)

1224
00:45:36,733 --> 00:45:37,733
- About a month later...

1225
00:45:37,767 --> 00:45:40,133
(chattering)

1226
00:45:40,167 --> 00:45:41,800
They were multiplying.

1227
00:45:41,833 --> 00:45:45,667
We did everything in the
world to exterminate them,

1228
00:45:45,700 --> 00:45:48,200
but (scoffing) no apparent luck.

1229
00:45:48,233 --> 00:45:49,800
- [Joel] Did you
try Roach Motel?

1230
00:45:49,833 --> 00:45:52,700
- We haven't seen any,
since daylight blinds them

1231
00:45:52,733 --> 00:45:56,067
and they forage only at
night when they're starving.

1232
00:45:56,100 --> 00:45:58,667
- [Tom Servo] Hmm, he's got
a few hairs left up there.

1233
00:45:58,700 --> 00:46:01,067
- But the fact that two of
them charged Ann and Jerry

1234
00:46:01,067 --> 00:46:04,567
at the gate last
evening indicates

1235
00:46:04,600 --> 00:46:06,867
that the available
food on the island

1236
00:46:06,900 --> 00:46:08,667
is nearing depletion.

1237
00:46:08,700 --> 00:46:11,500
- [Crow] Well, let's
have a food drive!

1238
00:46:11,533 --> 00:46:13,100
- Then what, Doctor?

1239
00:46:14,333 --> 00:46:16,733
- [Tom Servo] That's
it, don't you get it?

1240
00:46:16,767 --> 00:46:18,233
- It will take a couple days.

1241
00:46:18,267 --> 00:46:19,900
- What do we do
during that time?

1242
00:46:19,933 --> 00:46:21,067
- [Joel] Well,
there's always liquor.

1243
00:46:21,067 --> 00:46:22,833
- Stay indoors and
wait until it's over.

1244
00:46:22,867 --> 00:46:24,167
(suspenseful music)

1245
00:46:24,200 --> 00:46:25,067
- [Crow] Oh no!

1246
00:46:25,100 --> 00:46:26,367
They've got wire cutters!

1247
00:46:26,400 --> 00:46:29,067
- [Thorne] I guess some wires
are down on the transom.

1248
00:46:29,067 --> 00:46:30,833
- [Ann] I'm sorry.

1249
00:46:30,867 --> 00:46:34,067
- [Joel] Everyone, quick,
light your martinis!

1250
00:46:34,100 --> 00:46:34,967
- [Jerry] Mario!

1251
00:46:35,067 --> 00:46:36,067
Mario!

1252
00:46:36,067 --> 00:46:38,067
- [Mario] Si, senor?

1253
00:46:38,100 --> 00:46:39,300
- There's a light
in the pack there.

1254
00:46:39,333 --> 00:46:40,200
Light it and bring it in here.

1255
00:46:40,233 --> 00:46:43,167
- [Joel] I'll fix the drinks.

1256
00:46:43,200 --> 00:46:45,567
- [Jerry] Radford, light all
the candles you can find.

1257
00:46:45,600 --> 00:46:46,367
- All right, I will.
- And curse the darkness.

1258
00:46:46,400 --> 00:46:47,633
- [Craigis] Outside.

1259
00:46:47,667 --> 00:46:50,333
Can't get at it at night.

1260
00:46:50,367 --> 00:46:51,900
(suspenseful music)

1261
00:46:51,933 --> 00:46:53,133
- [Tom Servo] She's kind
of wearing fluffy culottes,

1262
00:46:53,167 --> 00:46:54,800
isn't she?
- Mm-hmm.

1263
00:46:54,833 --> 00:46:56,500
- A lantern will give you
all the light you need.

1264
00:46:56,533 --> 00:46:58,200
- [Joel] It's just as
light with candles.

1265
00:46:58,233 --> 00:47:00,133
- [Tom Servo] Hmm, amazing.

1266
00:47:00,167 --> 00:47:02,700
- [Tom Servo] This is
like a bad production

1267
00:47:02,733 --> 00:47:04,100
of a Chekhov play.

1268
00:47:05,233 --> 00:47:07,400
A wild geese.
- I don't like you.

1269
00:47:07,433 --> 00:47:11,067
(thunder rumbling)

1270
00:47:11,067 --> 00:47:13,133
- Well, Jerry, I
felt she was sincere

1271
00:47:13,167 --> 00:47:16,067
and made sense.
- Stop talking

1272
00:47:16,067 --> 00:47:17,867
and finish my pedicure.

1273
00:47:17,900 --> 00:47:19,167
- Wanting her father

1274
00:47:19,200 --> 00:47:20,167
to leave the island
with her makes sense?

1275
00:47:20,200 --> 00:47:22,533
It'd be much better
for the project

1276
00:47:22,567 --> 00:47:26,067
if I went with her
for a few days.

1277
00:47:26,067 --> 00:47:27,733
- [Tom Servo] Maybe
the Fanta suites.

1278
00:47:27,767 --> 00:47:29,400
- As a matter of fact,

1279
00:47:29,433 --> 00:47:30,833
I think I'll talk to
the doctor about that.

1280
00:47:30,867 --> 00:47:32,200
That might be the answer.

1281
00:47:32,233 --> 00:47:33,467
- Oh, great, it'll be a
20-minute scene, I'm sure.

1282
00:47:33,500 --> 00:47:35,467
- I don't think much
of this Thorne Sherman.

1283
00:47:35,500 --> 00:47:38,133
He looks to me like the
type that'd try anything.

1284
00:47:38,167 --> 00:47:39,133
- [Joel] Oh, really?

1285
00:47:39,167 --> 00:47:40,333
I think he's a really nice guy.

1286
00:47:40,367 --> 00:47:42,233
- That left-handed
dinner invitation.

1287
00:47:42,267 --> 00:47:44,333
That was just to keep me here
'til after dark, wasn't it?

1288
00:47:44,367 --> 00:47:46,300
- [Crow] I'm sorry, I was
sure you were left handed.

1289
00:47:46,333 --> 00:47:48,833
Can you eat with either hand?

1290
00:47:50,733 --> 00:47:53,067
- I wanted you here tonight.

1291
00:47:53,067 --> 00:47:55,733
I felt something terrible
was going to happen.

1292
00:47:55,767 --> 00:47:57,733
- Well, you still feel
the same way about it?

1293
00:47:57,767 --> 00:48:01,533
- Not as much, not since you're
here and know everything.

1294
00:48:01,567 --> 00:48:02,733
- [Joel] I don't
know everything.

1295
00:48:02,767 --> 00:48:05,467
I don't even know how fish work.

1296
00:48:05,500 --> 00:48:07,967
(suspenseful music)
- Oh, puppy!

1297
00:48:08,067 --> 00:48:09,167
Ooh, cute.

1298
00:48:09,200 --> 00:48:12,833
- No, not Fury!
- The biggest shrew of all.

1299
00:48:15,267 --> 00:48:16,233
- That was weird.

1300
00:48:16,267 --> 00:48:18,400
- I love an open
fire, don't you?

1301
00:48:18,433 --> 00:48:19,633
- Mm-hmm.

1302
00:48:19,667 --> 00:48:21,733
- [Tom Servo] Yeah,
they're great.

1303
00:48:21,767 --> 00:48:23,400
- [Joel] And I
love an open sewer.

1304
00:48:23,433 --> 00:48:25,967
- The wind has the
lonesomest sound.

1305
00:48:26,067 --> 00:48:27,467
Doesn't it?

1306
00:48:27,500 --> 00:48:30,400
- [Tom Servo] Wheat,
nothing but miles of wheat.

1307
00:48:30,433 --> 00:48:33,633
- Back in Sweden we don't
have a wind this strong.

1308
00:48:33,667 --> 00:48:37,233
- [Crow] Tell me
about your home world.

1309
00:48:37,267 --> 00:48:39,133
- You're a strange man, Thorne.

1310
00:48:39,167 --> 00:48:40,067
- [Joel] You're a queer duck.

1311
00:48:40,100 --> 00:48:41,400
- I never met anyone like you.

1312
00:48:41,433 --> 00:48:42,667
- [Thorne] Oh?

1313
00:48:43,933 --> 00:48:46,067
- You seem so disinterested
in everything.

1314
00:48:46,067 --> 00:48:47,300
- [Tom Servo] Uh-huh.

1315
00:48:47,333 --> 00:48:48,567
Look, you bore the
heck out of me.

1316
00:48:48,600 --> 00:48:49,200
- Aren't you the
least bit curious?

1317
00:48:49,233 --> 00:48:50,867
- [Joel] Yellow?

1318
00:48:50,900 --> 00:48:52,733
- Don't you wonder about the
unusual things around here?

1319
00:48:52,767 --> 00:48:54,567
- [Crow] Do you ever wonder
where felt comes from?

1320
00:48:54,600 --> 00:48:55,633
- The guns, the fence?

1321
00:48:55,667 --> 00:48:57,433
The shuttered windows?

1322
00:48:58,567 --> 00:49:00,100
My accent, anything?

1323
00:49:00,133 --> 00:49:02,133
- I'll tell you something.

1324
00:49:02,167 --> 00:49:05,233
I'm only interested in
anything that concerns me,

1325
00:49:05,267 --> 00:49:06,733
then I do something about it.

1326
00:49:06,767 --> 00:49:10,067
(horse neighing)

1327
00:49:10,067 --> 00:49:11,067
- [Joel] Did you
say Frau Blooker?

1328
00:49:11,067 --> 00:49:12,633
- You're gonna stay
with me tomorrow,

1329
00:49:12,667 --> 00:49:16,067
whether your father (horse
whinnying drowns out Thorne).

1330
00:49:17,733 --> 00:49:20,233
- [Tom Servo] He was Mister Ed.

1331
00:49:22,233 --> 00:49:23,500
- [Ann] Thorne, no!

1332
00:49:23,533 --> 00:49:24,833
- [Crow] No, don't
go in the bathroom!

1333
00:49:24,867 --> 00:49:26,667
We don't have a fan in there!

1334
00:49:26,700 --> 00:49:28,200
- [Ann] Out there!

1335
00:49:28,233 --> 00:49:31,233
(suspenseful music)

1336
00:49:32,833 --> 00:49:35,067
- [Tom Servo] Kim Curtis and
Kim Curtis in Double Impact!

1337
00:49:35,100 --> 00:49:36,400
- I couldn't take a chance.

1338
00:49:36,433 --> 00:49:38,800
He's fool enough to!

1339
00:49:38,833 --> 00:49:40,200
- Don't be stupid.

1340
00:49:40,233 --> 00:49:43,067
I had no intentions of going
there and opening that gate.

1341
00:49:43,067 --> 00:49:45,067
- [Joel] Oh, now my
mouth's bleeding, Burt!

1342
00:49:45,100 --> 00:49:46,867
- Did you hear it, Doctor?

1343
00:49:46,900 --> 00:49:48,533
It sounded like a human voice.

1344
00:49:48,567 --> 00:49:50,067
I was thinking of Rook.

1345
00:49:50,067 --> 00:49:50,967
- But the livestock.

1346
00:49:51,067 --> 00:49:52,733
The shrews got into the barn!

1347
00:49:52,767 --> 00:49:56,300
- [Crow] The shoes
got into the barn?

1348
00:49:56,333 --> 00:49:58,967
- [Tom Servo] I better put
some alcohol on this wound.

1349
00:49:59,067 --> 00:50:01,167
(dramatic music)

1350
00:50:01,200 --> 00:50:04,567
- Let me guess, the
shoe's in the bar.

1351
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:06,800
- What was it, Father?

1352
00:50:06,833 --> 00:50:08,000
- The livestock.

1353
00:50:08,033 --> 00:50:10,067
- [Joel] Live
socks, that's, huh?

1354
00:50:10,100 --> 00:50:11,400
- Mario, don't forget

1355
00:50:11,433 --> 00:50:13,900
to bar those doors.
* Booze glorious booze

1356
00:50:13,933 --> 00:50:15,333
- I put them in the barn myself.

1357
00:50:15,367 --> 00:50:16,833
- [Joel] What about
a bar mitzvah?

1358
00:50:16,867 --> 00:50:19,867
- They couldn't get
through the wood doors.

1359
00:50:19,900 --> 00:50:22,067
They dug through the dirt floor.

1360
00:50:22,100 --> 00:50:23,400
- [Thorne] Let me ask
you something, Doctor.

1361
00:50:23,433 --> 00:50:24,633
- [Tom Servo] Where
are the olives?

1362
00:50:24,667 --> 00:50:25,967
- How could you
expose all our lives,

1363
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:28,133
yourself included, with
those things out there?

1364
00:50:28,167 --> 00:50:29,367
- [Crow] Did he just
become more southern?

1365
00:50:29,400 --> 00:50:30,700
- All you had to do
was get the Coast Guard

1366
00:50:30,733 --> 00:50:32,033
or the Navy to come in
here and burn 'em out.

1367
00:50:32,067 --> 00:50:33,333
- [Tom Servo] Give me that.

1368
00:50:33,367 --> 00:50:34,900
- [Thorne] Those
things got loose.

1369
00:50:34,933 --> 00:50:38,000
- Any unusual experiment
can produce unusual results.

1370
00:50:38,033 --> 00:50:39,033
- [Crow] Hey, that was my drink!

1371
00:50:39,067 --> 00:50:41,400
- That's why I
chose this island.

1372
00:50:41,433 --> 00:50:42,733
It's isolated.

1373
00:50:42,767 --> 00:50:45,067
Miles of open water
in any direction.

1374
00:50:45,100 --> 00:50:47,333
- [Joel] Miles of cliff
in every direction.

1375
00:50:47,367 --> 00:50:49,700
- Our project is
privately financed.

1376
00:50:49,733 --> 00:50:51,033
- [Tom Servo] Here comes
Art Buckwald again.

1377
00:50:51,067 --> 00:50:54,033
- Not a product for the
government or military.

1378
00:50:54,067 --> 00:50:55,400
And the world is in no danger.

1379
00:50:55,433 --> 00:50:56,967
- [Crow] Oh, I've been
going through the script

1380
00:50:57,000 --> 00:50:58,967
and I think I'm in this scene.

1381
00:50:59,000 --> 00:51:00,733
- This species does not swim.

1382
00:51:00,767 --> 00:51:02,567
- [Joel] Yes, fishes do swim.

1383
00:51:02,600 --> 00:51:05,100
- This island is their world.

1384
00:51:06,167 --> 00:51:07,867
- [Tom Servo] And welcome to it.

1385
00:51:07,900 --> 00:51:10,167
- Very soon, right
here on this island.

1386
00:51:10,200 --> 00:51:11,800
- [Crow] Appearing
will be Don Ball!

1387
00:51:11,833 --> 00:51:14,367
- Miniature reproduction
of an overpopulated world.

1388
00:51:14,400 --> 00:51:15,900
- [Joel] That'll
bring in the tourists.

1389
00:51:15,933 --> 00:51:17,067
- You will see the importance

1390
00:51:17,067 --> 00:51:19,067
of what we're working to avoid.

1391
00:51:19,100 --> 00:51:21,067
- I'm not concerned
about all this theory.

1392
00:51:21,100 --> 00:51:22,733
What I'm concerned
about is our lives!

1393
00:51:22,767 --> 00:51:24,133
- [Joel] And my Rob Roy.

1394
00:51:24,167 --> 00:51:25,400
- Yeah, well, time to panic
when they run out of food

1395
00:51:25,433 --> 00:51:27,133
and go on a 24-hour forage.

1396
00:51:27,167 --> 00:51:29,733
- How do you know
they haven't already?

1397
00:51:29,767 --> 00:51:33,567
- [Joel] Yes, say yes
to making Martini Rossi.

1398
00:51:35,067 --> 00:51:36,867
- That source could've
been depleted.

1399
00:51:36,900 --> 00:51:38,067
- [Joel] Well, we
need more brandy.

1400
00:51:38,100 --> 00:51:40,133
Need more scotch.

1401
00:51:40,167 --> 00:51:41,200
We're out of vanilla extract.

1402
00:51:41,233 --> 00:51:44,067
- There's still some
food on this island

1403
00:51:44,100 --> 00:51:46,533
before there's a crisis.
- Gin.

1404
00:51:46,567 --> 00:51:47,333
Michelob.
- Where?

1405
00:51:47,367 --> 00:51:49,067
- With his big mouth.

1406
00:51:50,233 --> 00:51:51,200
- [Joel] What?

1407
00:51:51,233 --> 00:51:52,533
It's paid for.

1408
00:51:52,567 --> 00:51:53,700
- Don't worry about
them digging in here.

1409
00:51:53,733 --> 00:51:56,067
The floors are tile.

1410
00:51:56,100 --> 00:51:57,333
- [Thorne] The walls
aren't, Doctor.

1411
00:51:57,367 --> 00:51:58,333
They're adobe.

1412
00:51:58,367 --> 00:51:59,567
- [Joel] Kill us!

1413
00:51:59,600 --> 00:52:01,567
- Our safest bet
would be on that boat.

1414
00:52:01,600 --> 00:52:03,233
- You may be right.

1415
00:52:03,267 --> 00:52:04,833
- [Tom Servo] I may be crazy.

1416
00:52:04,867 --> 00:52:07,067
- [Dr. Craigis] You can
reach your boat in daylight.

1417
00:52:07,067 --> 00:52:08,367
The shrews will
gorge themselves.

1418
00:52:08,400 --> 00:52:10,133
- [Crow] Booze is for healing.

1419
00:52:10,167 --> 00:52:13,067
- That should keep them
lethargic for several hours.

1420
00:52:13,100 --> 00:52:14,667
- There's no sense
looking at the dark side.

1421
00:52:14,700 --> 00:52:18,667
- And there's no sense
minimizing a serious situation.

1422
00:52:18,700 --> 00:52:21,200
It leaves you completely
unprepared to cope with it.

1423
00:52:21,233 --> 00:52:22,533
- [Crow] Hey!

1424
00:52:22,567 --> 00:52:23,800
- [Joel] Well you
took mine earlier.

1425
00:52:23,833 --> 00:52:25,067
- Now we certainly
can't make it tonight.

1426
00:52:25,067 --> 00:52:27,067
That's out of the question.
- Maybe tomorrow.

1427
00:52:27,100 --> 00:52:28,467
Someday, and soon!

1428
00:52:28,500 --> 00:52:30,833
- [Joel] Synchronized drinking!

1429
00:52:30,867 --> 00:52:31,733
- I have it.

1430
00:52:31,767 --> 00:52:33,133
We'll get some sleep tonight.

1431
00:52:33,167 --> 00:52:35,067
But we'll have a watch,
an hour and a half apiece.

1432
00:52:35,100 --> 00:52:37,067
Doctor, you go
first, then Radford.

1433
00:52:37,067 --> 00:52:38,067
- [Joel] I got mentioned.

1434
00:52:38,100 --> 00:52:39,067
- Jerry, and then me.

1435
00:52:39,100 --> 00:52:42,067
- [Joel] They're setting
up a Quarters game.

1436
00:52:42,067 --> 00:52:43,067
- Make sure they're
locked good and tight.

1437
00:52:43,067 --> 00:52:44,867
Check all these walls.

1438
00:52:44,900 --> 00:52:47,233
- [Crow] And roll
the brewmeister in
here so it's closer.

1439
00:52:47,267 --> 00:52:48,500
- Come now.

1440
00:52:48,533 --> 00:52:50,133
- No, I think I'll
stay here by the fire.

1441
00:52:50,167 --> 00:52:52,467
I'd feel safer.
- And closer to the bar.

1442
00:52:52,500 --> 00:52:53,967
- I couldn't stand it
in that room alone.

1443
00:52:54,067 --> 00:52:56,200
- All right, as you wish.

1444
00:52:56,233 --> 00:52:58,067
But try to get some sleep.

1445
00:52:58,067 --> 00:53:01,067
- [Tom Servo] But fart
and get some sleep?

1446
00:53:01,067 --> 00:53:04,633
- You know what I've been
thinking about, Mario?

1447
00:53:04,667 --> 00:53:06,300
- [Tom Servo] Socks,
I don't know why.

1448
00:53:06,333 --> 00:53:09,167
- Ann and that Captain.

1449
00:53:09,200 --> 00:53:10,967
- [Joel] Oh, the space
captain fantasy again.

1450
00:53:11,067 --> 00:53:12,133
- You and me.

1451
00:53:12,167 --> 00:53:14,067
- [Crow] We will need
a bigger room, then.

1452
00:53:14,067 --> 00:53:17,233
- Imagine an intelligent
girl like her

1453
00:53:17,267 --> 00:53:20,500
going for a common
sea tramp like him.

1454
00:53:23,400 --> 00:53:25,533
- [Tom Servo] Imagine in
how much detail, senor?

1455
00:53:25,567 --> 00:53:27,167
- I know why!

1456
00:53:27,200 --> 00:53:28,567
You do too.

1457
00:53:28,600 --> 00:53:29,700
- [Crow] Por que?

1458
00:53:29,733 --> 00:53:31,333
- In case anything goes wrong,

1459
00:53:31,367 --> 00:53:33,467
she's looking out
for her own hide.

1460
00:53:33,500 --> 00:53:34,967
- [Crow] 'Kay, it's typical.

1461
00:53:35,067 --> 00:53:37,333
- That's why I keep
thinking about you and me.

1462
00:53:37,367 --> 00:53:38,867
- [Joel] And Bobby McGee?

1463
00:53:38,900 --> 00:53:40,833
- Anything goes wrong?

1464
00:53:40,867 --> 00:53:43,133
- [Tom Servo] I'm not
Lisa, my name is Mario.

1465
00:53:43,167 --> 00:53:46,233
- We're gonna outlast 'em all.

1466
00:53:46,267 --> 00:53:48,533
(wind whooshing)
(thunder crashing)

1467
00:53:48,567 --> 00:53:51,233
- [Joel] Sinners repent!

1468
00:53:51,267 --> 00:53:54,600
- [Crow] Let's get
out of here, Scooby!

1469
00:53:55,667 --> 00:53:57,167
